window.KF_DATA = {"brand":"Kill Fee","system":"Daggerheart","setting":"Night City","level":1,"modifiers":[2,1,1,0,0,-1],"traits":[{"name":"Movement","was":"Agility","verbs":"Sprint, Leap, Maneuver"},{"name":"Body","was":"Strength","verbs":"Lift, Smash, Grapple"},{"name":"Reflexes","was":"Finesse","verbs":"Control, Hide, Tinker"},{"name":"Empathy","was":"Instinct","verbs":"Perceive, Sense, Navigate"},{"name":"Cool","was":"Presence","verbs":"Charm, Perform, Deceive"},{"name":"Intelligence","was":"Knowledge","verbs":"Recall, Analyze, Comprehend"}],"classes":[{"name":"Bard","was":"Bard","hf":false,"description":"Bards are the most charismatic people in all the realms. Members of this class are masters of captivation and specialize in a variety of performance types, including singing, playing musical instruments, weaving tales, or telling jokes. Whether performing for an audience or speaking to an individual, bards thrive in social situations. Members of this profession bond and train at schools or guilds, but a current of egotism runs through those of the bardic persuasion. While they may be the most likely class to bring people together, a bard of ill temper can just as easily tear a party apart.","domains":["Grace","Codex"],"evasion":10,"hp":5,"class_items":"A demo chip nobody's played yet, or a press pass that expired years ago","background_questions":["Who taught you to work a crowd, and what did it cost them?","You were somebody's voice once — a band, a station, a cause. Who shut you down, and why do you keep going?","There's a song, a story or a broadcast with your name on it that you'd give anything to take back. What was it?"],"connections":["What did I say about you on air, and why did you forgive me?","Which of my songs is about you?","Why do you always stand near the door when I perform?"],"book_items":"A romance novel or a letter never opened","features":[{"name":"Make a Scene","text":"Spend 3 Hope to temporarily *Distract* a target within Close range, giving them a -2 penalty to their Difficulty.","was":"Make a Scene"},{"name":"Rally","text":"Once per session, describe how you rally the party and give yourself and each of your allies a Rally Die. At level 1, your Rally Die is a **d6**. A PC can spend their Rally Die to roll it, adding the result to their action roll, reaction roll, damage roll, or to clear a number of Stress equal to the result. At the end of each session, clear all unspent Rally Dice.\nAt level 5, your Rally Die increases to a **d8**.","was":"Rally"}],"subclasses":[{"name":"Rockerboy","was":"Troubadour","tropes":"Frontman of a Samurai-style band, a club act with a following, a street musician the gangs leave alone.","note":"","description":"*Play the Rockerboy if you want to play music to bolster your allies.*","spellcast_trait":"Cool","foundation":[{"name":"Gifted Performer","text":"Describe how you perform for others. You can play each song once per long rest:\n• **Relaxing Song:** You and all allies within Close range clear a Hit Point.\n• **Epic Song:** Make a target within Close range temporarily *Vulnerable*.\n• **Heartbreaking Song:** You and all allies within Close range gain a Hope.","was":"Gifted Performer"}],"specialization":[{"name":"Maestro","text":"Your rallying songs steel the courage of those who listen. When you give a Rally Die to an ally, they can gain a Hope or clear a Stress.","was":"Maestro"}]},{"name":"Media","was":"Wordsmith","tropes":"N54 stringer, screamsheet writer, streamer with an audience that shows up.","note":"","description":"*Play the Media if you want to use clever wordplay and captivate crowds.*","spellcast_trait":"Cool","foundation":[{"name":"Rousing Speech","text":"Once per long rest, you can give a heartfelt, inspiring speech. All allies within Far range clear 2 Stress.","was":"Rousing Speech"},{"name":"Heart of a Poet","text":"After you make an action roll to impress, persuade, or offend someone, you can **spend a Hope** to add a **d4** to the roll.","was":"Heart of a Poet"}],"specialization":[{"name":"Eloquent","text":"Your moving words boost morale. Once per session, when you encourage an ally, you can do one of the following:\n• Allow them to find a mundane object or tool they need.\n• Help an Ally without spending Hope.\n• Give them an additional downtime move during their next rest.","was":"Eloquent"}]}],"guide":{"traits":{"Movement":0,"Body":-1,"Reflexes":1,"Empathy":0,"Cool":2,"Intelligence":1},"primary":"Nanoblade","secondary":"Switchblade","armor":"Ballistic Jacket"}},{"name":"Druid","was":"Druid","hf":false,"description":"Becoming a druid is more than an occupation; it's a calling for those who wish to learn from and protect the magic of the wilderness. While one might underestimate a gentle druid who practices the often-quiet work of cultivating flora, druids who channel the untamed forces of nature are terrifying to behold. Druids cultivate their abilities in small groups, often connected by a specific ethos or locale, but some choose to work alone. Through years of study and dedication, druids can learn to transform into beasts and shape nature itself.","domains":["Sage","Arcana"],"evasion":10,"hp":6,"class_items":"A vial of something you cooked yourself, or a dead runner's deck you never wiped","background_questions":["What did you change about yourself first — your code or your blood — and who was there when you did?","Something you built or brewed got loose. What was it, and what did it do?","Who in Night City knows what you really are, and why haven't they sold that yet?"],"connections":["What did I give you that you still haven't asked about?","Which version of me do you trust — before or after?","Why did you stay when the comedown hit?"],"book_items":"A small bag of rocks and bones or a strange pendant found in the dirt","features":[{"name":"Evolution","text":"Spend 3 Hope to transform into a Beastform without marking a Stress. When you do, choose one trait to raise by +1 until you drop out of that Beastform.","was":"Evolution"},{"name":"Beastform","text":"Mark a Stress to magically transform into a creature of your tier or lower from the Beastform list. You can drop out of this form at any time. While transformed, you can't use weapons or cast spells from domain cards, but you can still use other features or abilities you have access to. Spells you cast before you transform stay active and last for their normal duration, and you can talk and communicate as normal. Additionally, you gain the Beastform's features, add their Evasion bonus to your Evasion, and use the trait specified in their statistics for your attack. While you're in a Beastform, your armor becomes part of your body and you mark Armor Slots as usual; when you drop out of a Beastform, those marked Armor Slots remain marked. If you mark your last Hit Point, you automatically drop out of this form.","was":"Beastform"},{"name":"Wildtouch","text":"You can perform harmless, subtle effects that involve nature—such as causing a flower to rapidly grow, summoning a slight gust of wind, or starting a campfire—at will.","was":"Wildtouch"}],"subclasses":[{"name":"Daemon Rider","was":"Warden of the Elements","tropes":"Lunar netrunner, ICE-breaker, Voodoo Boys-adjacent — Beastform is digital shapeshifting: crawler forms to slip ICE, hunter-killer forms to fight other runners.","note":"","description":"Play the Daemon Rider if you want to embody the natural elements of the wild.","spellcast_trait":"Empathy","foundation":[{"name":"Elemental Incarnation","text":"Mark a Stress to Channel one of the following elements until you take Severe damage or until your next rest:\n• Fire: When an adversary within Melee range deals damage to you, they take 1d10 electronic damage.\n• Earth: Gain a bonus to your damage thresholds equal to your Proficiency.\n• Water: When you deal damage to an adversary within Melee range, all other adversaries within Very Close range must mark a Stress.\n• Air: You can hover, gaining advantage on Movement Rolls.","was":"Elemental Incarnation"}],"specialization":[{"name":"Elemental Aura","text":"Once per rest while Channeling, you can assume an aura matching your element. The aura affects targets within Close range until your Channeling ends.\n• Fire: When an adversary marks 1 or more Hit Points, they must also mark a Stress.\n• Earth: Your allies gain a +1 bonus to Body.\n• Water: When an adversary deals damage to you, you can mark a Stress to move them anywhere within Very Close range of where they are.\n• Air: When you or an ally takes damage from an attack beyond Melee range, reduce the damage by 1d8.","was":"Elemental Aura"}]},{"name":"Biohacker","was":"Warden of Renewal","tropes":"Chem cook, back-room ripper's partner, the no-chrome edge — Beastform is a chemically altered state (fast, monstrous, numb to pain) with a comedown.","note":"","description":"Play the Biohacker if you want to use powerful magic to heal your party.","spellcast_trait":"Empathy","foundation":[{"name":"Clarity of Nature","text":"Once per long rest, you can create a space of natural serenity within Close range. When you spend a few minutes resting within the space, clear Stress equal to your Empathy, distributed as you choose between you and your allies.","was":"Clarity of Nature"},{"name":"Regeneration","text":"Touch a creature and spend 3 Hope. That creature clears 1d4 Hit Points.","was":"Regeneration"}],"specialization":[{"name":"Regenerative Reach","text":"You can target creatures within Very Close range with your “Regeneration” feature.","was":"Regenerative Reach"},{"name":"Warden's Protection","text":"Once per long rest, spend 2 Hope to clear 2 Hit Points on 1d4 allies within Close range.","was":"Warden's Protection"}]}],"guide":{"traits":{"Movement":1,"Body":0,"Reflexes":1,"Empathy":2,"Cool":-1,"Intelligence":0},"primary":"Shock Baton","secondary":"Ballistic Shield","armor":"Kevlar Vest"}},{"name":"Guardian","was":"Guardian","hf":false,"description":"The title of guardian represents an array of martial professions, speaking more to their moral compass and unshakeable fortitude than the means by which they fight. While many guardians join groups of militants for either a country or cause, they’re more likely to follow those few they truly care for, majority be damned. Guardians are known for fighting with remarkable ferocity even against overwhelming odds, defending their cohort above all else. Woe betide those who harm the ally of a guardian, as the guardian will answer this injury in kind.","domains":["Valor","Blade"],"evasion":9,"hp":7,"class_items":"A client's lanyard you kept, or a dented Trauma Team patch that isn't yours","background_questions":["Who did you fail to protect, and why do you still carry their picture?","What are you guarding right now that you can't tell the crew about?","Which part of you does the chrome cover, and who saw it before it was covered?"],"connections":["What did I take for you, and why don't you ever mention it?","Who did you ask me to protect, and did I?","Why do I let you walk in front?"],"book_items":"A totem from your mentor or a secret key","features":[{"name":"Frontline Tank","text":"Spend 3 Hope to clear 2 Armor Slots.","was":"Frontline Tank"},{"name":"Unstoppable","text":"Once per long rest, you can become *Unstoppable*. You gain an Unstoppable Die. At level 1, your Unstoppable Die is a **d4**. Place it on your character sheet in the space provided, starting with the 1 value facing up. After you make a damage roll that deals 1 or more Hit Points to a target, increase the Unstoppable Die value by one. When the die’s value would exceed its maximum value or when the scene ends, remove the die and drop out of *Unstoppable*. At level 5, your Unstoppable Die increases to a **d6**.\nWhile *Unstoppable*, you gain the following benefits:\n• You reduce the severity of physical damage by one threshold (Severe to Major, Major to Minor, Minor to None).\n• You add the current value of the Unstoppable Die to your damage roll.\n• You can’t be *Restrained* or *Vulnerable*.\n**Tip:** *If your Unstoppable Die is a d4 and the 4 is currently facing up, you remove the die the next time you would increase it. However, if your Unstoppable Die has increased to a d6 and the 4 is currently facing up, you’ll turn it to 5 the next time you would increase it. In this case, you’ll remove the die after you would need to increase it higher than 6.*","was":"Unstoppable"}],"subclasses":[{"name":"Bodyguard","was":"Stalwart","tropes":"Corporate close protection, Arasaka security detail, ex-Trauma Team — the one who soaks hits for the client.","note":"","description":"*Play the Bodyguard if you want to take heavy blows and keep fighting.*","spellcast_trait":"","foundation":[{"name":"Unwavering","text":"Gain a permanent +1 bonus to your damage thresholds.","was":"Unwavering"},{"name":"Iron Will","text":"When you take physical damage, you can **mark an additional Armor Slot** to reduce the severity.","was":"Iron Will"}],"specialization":[{"name":"Unrelenting","text":"Gain a permanent +2 bonus to your damage thresholds.","was":"Unrelenting"},{"name":"Partners-in-Arms","text":"When an ally within Very Close range takes damage, you can **mark an Armor Slot** to reduce the severity by one threshold.","was":"Partners-in-Arms"}]},{"name":"Heavy","was":"Vengeance","tropes":"Maelstrom muscle, 6th Street, gang enforcer — anyone who hurts the team pays for it.","note":"","description":"*Play the Vengeance if you want to strike down enemies who harm you or your allies.*","spellcast_trait":"","foundation":[{"name":"At Ease","text":"Gain an additional Stress slot.","was":"At Ease"},{"name":"Revenge","text":"When an adversary within Melee range succeeds on an attack against you, you can **mark 2 Stress** to force the attacker to mark a Hit Point.","was":"Revenge"}],"specialization":[{"name":"Act of Reprisal","text":"When an adversary damages an ally within Melee range, you gain a +1 bonus to your Proficiency for the next successful attack you make against that adversary.","was":"Act of Reprisal"}]}],"guide":{"traits":{"Movement":1,"Body":2,"Reflexes":-1,"Empathy":0,"Cool":1,"Intelligence":0},"primary":"Fire Axe","secondary":"","armor":"Tactical Vest"}},{"name":"Ranger","was":"Ranger","hf":false,"description":"Rangers are highly skilled hunters who, despite their martial abilities, rarely lend their skills to an army. Through mastery of the body and a deep understanding of the wilderness, rangers become sly tacticians, pursuing their quarry with cunning and patience. Many rangers track and fight alongside an animal companion with whom they've forged a powerful spiritual bond. By honing their skills in the wild, rangers become expert trackers, as likely to ensnare their foes in a trap as they are to assail them head-on.","domains":["Bone","Sage"],"evasion":12,"hp":6,"class_items":"Keys to a ride that isn't yours anymore, or a logbook of plates and faces","background_questions":["What's outside the city that you keep going back to, and who's still out there?","Who or what are you tracking that nobody's paying you for?","You've driven away from something you shouldn't have. What was it?"],"connections":["Where did I find you, and what were you doing out there?","What do you leave in my ride, and why don't I throw it out?","Who did I track down for you, and did you thank me?"],"book_items":"A trophy from your first kill or a seemingly broken compass","features":[{"name":"Hold Them Off","text":"Spend 3 Hope when you succeed on an attack with a weapon to use that same roll against two additional adversaries within range of the attack.","was":"Hold Them Off"},{"name":"Ranger's Focus","text":"Spend a Hope and make an attack against a target. On a success, deal your attack's normal damage and temporarily make the attack's target your *Focus*. Until this feature ends or you make a different creature your *Focus*, you gain the following benefits against your *Focus*:\n• You know precisely what direction they are in.\n• When you deal damage to them, they must mark a Stress.\n• When you fail an attack against them, you can end your Ranger's Focus feature to reroll your Duality Dice.","was":"Ranger's Focus"}],"subclasses":[{"name":"Nomad","was":"Beastbound","tropes":"Aldecaldos, Badlands convoy — the companion is a vehicle, a dog, or a drone.","note":"","description":"Play the Nomad if you want to form a deep bond with an animal ally.","spellcast_trait":"Movement","foundation":[{"name":"Companion","text":"You have an animal companion of your choice (at the GM's discretion). They stay by your side unless you tell them otherwise.\n\nTake the Ranger Companion sheet. When you level up your character, choose a level-up option for your companion from this sheet as well.","was":"Companion"}],"specialization":[{"name":"Expert Training","text":"Choose an additional level-up option for your companion.","was":"Expert Training"},{"name":"Battle-Bonded","text":"When an adversary attacks you while they're within your companion's Melee range, you gain a +2 bonus to your Evasion against the attack.","was":"Battle-Bonded"}]},{"name":"Bounty Hunter","was":"Wayfinder","tropes":"Skip tracer, NetWatch tracker, braindance detective.","note":"","description":"Play the Bounty Hunter if you want to hunt your prey and strike with deadly force.","spellcast_trait":"Movement","foundation":[{"name":"Ruthless Predator","text":"When you make a damage roll, you can mark a Stress to gain a +1 bonus to your Proficiency. Additionally, when you deal Severe damage to an adversary, they must mark a Stress.","was":"Ruthless Predator"},{"name":"Path Forward","text":"When you're traveling to a place you've previously visited or you carry an object that has been at the location before, you can identify the shortest, most direct path to your destination.","was":"Path Forward"}],"specialization":[{"name":"Elusive Predator","text":"When your *Focus* makes an attack against you, you gain a +2 bonus to your Evasion against the attack.","was":"Elusive Predator"}]}],"guide":{"traits":{"Movement":2,"Body":0,"Reflexes":1,"Empathy":1,"Cool":-1,"Intelligence":0},"primary":"Lexington SMG","secondary":"","armor":"Kevlar Vest"}},{"name":"Rogue","was":"Rogue","hf":false,"description":"Rogues are scoundrels, often in both attitude and practice. Broadly known as liars and thieves, the best among this class move through the world anonymously. Utilizing their sharp wits and blades, rogues trick their foes through social manipulation as easily as breaking locks, climbing through windows, or dealing underhanded blows. These masters of magical craft manipulate shadow and movement, adding an array of useful and deadly tools to their repertoire. Rogues frequently establish guilds to meet future accomplices, hire out jobs, and hone secret skills, proving that there’s honor among thieves for those who know where to look.","domains":["Midnight","Grace"],"evasion":12,"hp":6,"class_items":"A burner with one saved number, or a keycard to a building you've never admitted entering","background_questions":["Who's the one person in Night City who could end you with a phone call, and why haven't they?","What's the job you walked away from, and what did you take with you?","Which district knows your face, and which name do they know it by?"],"connections":["What do you know about me that you've never put a price on?","Which name do you call me by, and why that one?","What did you see me take?"],"book_items":"A set of forgery tools or a grappling hook","features":[{"name":"Rogue’s Dodge","text":"Spend 3 Hope to gain a +2 bonus to your Evasion until the next time an attack succeeds against you. Otherwise, this bonus lasts until your next rest.","was":"Rogue’s Dodge"},{"name":"Cloaked","text":"Any time you would be *Hidden*, you are instead *Cloaked*. In addition to the benefits of the *Hidden* condition, while *Cloaked* you remain unseen if you are stationary when an adversary moves to where they would normally see you. After you make an attack or end a move within line of sight of an adversary, you are no longer *Cloaked*.","was":"Cloaked"},{"name":"Sneak Attack","text":"When you succeed on an attack while *Cloaked* or while an ally is within Melee range of your target, add a number of **d6s** equal to your tier to your damage roll.\nLevel 1 → Tier 1\nLevels 2–4 → Tier 2\nLevels 5–7 → Tier 3\nLevels 8–10 → Tier 4","was":"Sneak Attack"}],"subclasses":[{"name":"Infiltrator","was":"Nightwalker","tropes":"Stealth solo, optical-camo ghost, corporate thief — the shadow abilities are camo and reflex boosters.","note":"","description":"*Play the Infiltrator if you want to manipulate shadows to maneuver through the environment.*","spellcast_trait":"Reflexes","foundation":[{"name":"Shadow Stepper","text":"You can move from shadow to shadow. When you move into an area of darkness or a shadow cast by another creature or object, you can **mark a Stress** to disappear from where you are and reappear inside another shadow within Far range. When you reappear, you are *Cloaked*.","was":"Shadow Stepper"}],"specialization":[{"name":"Dark Cloud","text":"Make a **Quickhack Roll (15)**. On a success, create a temporary dark cloud that covers any area within Close range. Anyone in this cloud can’t see outside of it, and anyone outside of it can’t see in. You’re considered *Cloaked* from any adversary for whom the cloud blocks line of sight.","was":"Dark Cloud"},{"name":"Adrenaline","text":"While you're *Vulnerable*, add your level to your damage rolls.","was":"Adrenaline"}]},{"name":"Fixer","was":"Syndicate","tropes":"Kabuki fixer, Afterlife regular, a contact in every district — the network is the augmentation.","note":"","description":"*Play the Syndicate if you want to have a web of contacts everywhere you go.*","spellcast_trait":"Reflexes","foundation":[{"name":"Well-Connected","text":"When you arrive in a prominent town or environment, you know somebody who calls this place home. Give them a name, note how you think they could be useful, and choose one fact from the following list:\n\n• They owe me a favor, but they’ll be hard to find.\n\n• They’re going to ask for something in exchange.\n\n• They’re always in a great deal of trouble.\n\n• We used to be together. It’s a long story.\n\n• We didn’t part on great terms.","was":"Well-Connected"}],"specialization":[{"name":"Contacts Everywhere","text":"Once per session, you can briefly call on a shady contact. Choose one of the following benefits and describe what brought them here to help you in this moment:\n\n• They provide 1 handful of gold, a unique tool, or a mundane object that the situation requires.\n\n• On your next action roll, their help provides a +3 bonus to the result of your Hope or Fear Die.\n\n• The next time you deal damage, they snipe from the shadows, adding **2d8** to your damage roll.","was":"Contacts Everywhere"}]}],"guide":{"traits":{"Movement":1,"Body":-1,"Reflexes":2,"Empathy":0,"Cool":1,"Intelligence":0},"primary":"Combat Knife","secondary":"Switchblade","armor":"Ballistic Jacket"}},{"name":"Seraph","was":"Seraph","hf":false,"description":"Seraphs are divine fighters and healers imbued with sacred purpose. A wide array of deities exist within the realms, and thus numerous kinds of seraphs are appointed by these gods. Their ethos traditionally aligns with the domain or goals of their god, such as defending the weak, exacting vengeance, protecting a land or artifact, or upholding a particular faith. Some seraphs ally themselves with an army or locale, much to the satisfaction of their rulers, but other crusaders fight in opposition to the follies of the Mortal Realm. It is better to be a seraph’s ally than their enemy, as they are terrifying foes to those who defy their purpose.","domains":["Splendor","Valor"],"evasion":9,"hp":7,"class_items":"A med-kit with one slot always empty, or a badge you turned in and kept anyway","background_questions":["Who did you swear to save, and who made you break it?","Whose life did you decide wasn't worth the premium?","What do you still carry from the service — a scar, a habit, a rule you can't stop following?"],"connections":["Which of you did I pull out, and what did it cost?","Why do you keep calling me doc?","What did you ask me to let go of, and did I?"],"book_items":"A bundle of offerings or a sigil of your god","features":[{"name":"Life Support","text":"Spend 3 Hope to clear a Hit Point on an ally within Close range.","was":"Life Support"},{"name":"Prayer Dice","text":"At the beginning of each session, roll a number of d4s equal to your subclass’s Netrun trait and place them on your character sheet in the space provided. These are your Prayer Dice. You can spend any number of Prayer Dice to aid yourself or an ally within Far range. You can use a spent die’s value to reduce incoming damage, add to a roll’s result after the roll is made, or gain Hope equal to the result. At the end of each session, clear all unspent Prayer Dice.","was":"Prayer Dice"}],"subclasses":[{"name":"Medtech","was":"Divine Wielder","tropes":"Trauma Team, combat medic, ripperdoc's partner — Prayer Dice are a stim or med-kit pool.","note":"","description":"Play the Medtech if you want to dominate the battlefield with a legendary weapon.","spellcast_trait":"Body","foundation":[{"name":"Spirit Weapon","text":"When you have an equipped weapon with a range of Melee or Very Close, it can fly from your hand to attack an adversary within Close range and then return to you. You can mark a Stress to target an additional adversary within range with the same attack roll.","was":"Spirit Weapon"},{"name":"Sparing Touch","text":"Once per long rest, touch a creature and clear 2 Hit Points or 2 Stress from them.","was":"Sparing Touch"}],"specialization":[{"name":"Devout","text":"When you roll your Prayer Dice, you can roll an additional die and discard the lowest result. Additionally, you can use your “Sparing Touch” feature twice instead of once per long rest.","was":"Devout"}]},{"name":"Lawman","was":"Winged Sentinel","tropes":"MAXTAC, NCPD, corporate response team.","note":"Wings of Light flies and carries people — AV drop or jet rig, the GM rules which.","description":"Play the Lawman if you want to take flight and strike crushing blows from the sky.","spellcast_trait":"Body","foundation":[{"name":"Wings of Light","text":"You can fly. While flying, you can do the following:\n• Mark a Stress to pick up and carry another willing creature approximately your size or smaller.\n• Spend a Hope to deal an extra 1d8 damage on a successful attack.","was":"Wings of Light"}],"specialization":[{"name":"Ethereal Visage","text":"Your supernatural visage strikes awe and fear. While flying, you have advantage on Cool Rolls. When you succeed with Hope on a Cool Roll, you can remove a Fear from the GM’s Fear pool instead of gaining Hope.","was":"Ethereal Visage"}]}],"guide":{"traits":{"Movement":0,"Body":2,"Reflexes":0,"Empathy":1,"Cool":1,"Intelligence":-1},"primary":"Arc Hatchet","secondary":"Ballistic Shield","armor":"Tactical Vest"}},{"name":"Sorcerer","was":"Sorcerer","hf":false,"description":"Not all innate magic users choose to hone their craft, but those who do can become powerful sorcerers. The gifts of these wielders are passed down through families, even if the family is unaware of or reluctant to practice them. A sorcerer’s abilities can range from the elemental to the illusionary and beyond, and many practitioners band together into collectives based on their talents. The act of becoming a formidable sorcerer is not the practice of acquiring power, but learning to cultivate and control the power one already possesses. The magic of a misguided or undisciplined sorcerer is a dangerous force indeed.","domains":["Arcana","Midnight"],"evasion":10,"hp":6,"class_items":"A soldering iron that's never let you down, or a prototype that shouldn't work","background_questions":["What did you build that nobody understands, including you?","Who wanted your work badly enough to hurt you for it?","Your rig fails sometimes. What happened the last time it did?"],"connections":["What did I build for you, and does it still work?","Why did you stay in the room when it caught fire?","What do you wish I'd stop testing on you?"],"book_items":"A whispering orb or a family heirloom","features":[{"name":"Volatile Magic","text":"Spend 3 Hope to reroll any number of your damage dice on an attack that deals electronic damage.","was":"Volatile Magic"},{"name":"Arcane Sense","text":"You can sense the presence of magical people and objects within Close range.","was":"Arcane Sense"},{"name":"Minor Illusion","text":"Make a Quickhack Roll (10). On a success, you create a minor visual illusion no larger than yourself within Close range. This illusion is convincing to anyone at Close range or farther.","was":"Minor Illusion"},{"name":"Channel Raw Power","text":"Once per long rest, you can place a domain card from your loadout into your vault and choose to either:\n• Gain Hope equal to the level of the card.\n• Enhance a spell that deals damage, gaining a bonus to your damage roll equal to twice the level of the card.","was":"Channel Raw Power"}],"subclasses":[{"name":"Tech (Elementalist)","was":"Elemental Origin","tropes":"Street techie, Arroyo garage, drone and rig builder — your element is your rig's specialty.","note":"","description":"Play the Tech (Elementalist) if you want to channel raw magic to take the shape of a particular element.","spellcast_trait":"Empathy","foundation":[{"name":"Elementalist","text":"Choose one of the following elements at character creation: air, earth, fire, lightning, water.\n\nYou can shape this element into harmless effects. Additionally, spend a Hope and describe how your control over this element helps an action roll you’re about to make, then either gain a +2 bonus to the roll or a +3 bonus to the roll’s damage.","was":"Elementalist"}],"specialization":[{"name":"Natural Evasion","text":"You can call forth your element to protect you from harm. When an attack roll against you succeeds, you can mark a Stress and describe how you use your element to defend you. When you do, roll a d6 and add its result to your Evasion against the attack.","was":"Natural Evasion"}]},{"name":"Tech (Overclock)","was":"Primal Origin","tropes":"Street techie who pushes everything past spec — extends, boosts and burns out their own gear.","note":"","description":"Play the Tech (Overclock) if you want to extend the versatility of your spells in powerful ways.","spellcast_trait":"Empathy","foundation":[{"name":"Manipulate Magic","text":"Your primal origin allows you to modify the essence of magic itself. After you cast a spell or make an attack using a weapon that deals electronic damage, you can mark a Stress to do one of the following:\n• Extend the spell or attack’s reach by one range\n• Gain a +2 bonus to the action roll’s result\n• Double a damage die of your choice\n• Hit an additional target within range","was":"Manipulate Magic"}],"specialization":[{"name":"Enchanted Aid","text":"You can enhance the magic of others with your essence. When you Help an Ally with a Quickhack Roll, you can roll a d8 as your advantage die. Once per long rest, after an ally has made a Quickhack Roll with your help, you can swap the results of their Duality Dice.","was":"Enchanted Aid"}]}],"guide":{"traits":{"Movement":0,"Body":-1,"Reflexes":1,"Empathy":2,"Cool":1,"Intelligence":0},"primary":"Widow Maker Tech Rifle","secondary":"","armor":"Ballistic Jacket"}},{"name":"Warrior","was":"Warrior","hf":false,"description":"Becoming a warrior requires years, often a lifetime, of training and dedication to the mastery of weapons and violence. While many who seek to fight hone only their strength, warriors understand the importance of an agile body and mind, making them some of the most sought-after fighters across the realms. Frequently, warriors find employment within an army, a band of mercenaries, or even a royal guard, but their potential is wasted in any position where they cannot continue to improve and expand their skills. Warriors are known to have a favored weapon; to come between them and their blade would be a grievous mistake.","domains":["Blade","Bone"],"evasion":11,"hp":6,"class_items":"A dog tag that isn't yours, or a braindance of your best fight","background_questions":["What war did Night City put you through, and whose side were you on?","Who taught you that the first move is the one that matters, and where are they now?","There's a fight you still watch on braindance. What was at stake?"],"connections":["What did you do when I didn't get back up?","Which of us would win, and why have we never found out?","What do I owe you from the last job?"],"book_items":"The drawing of a lover or a sharpening stone","features":[{"name":"No Mercy","text":"Spend 3 Hope to gain a +1 bonus to your attack rolls until your next rest.","was":"No Mercy"},{"name":"Attack of Opportunity","text":"If an adversary within Melee range attempts to leave that range, make a reaction roll using a trait of your choice against their Difficulty. Choose one effect on a success, or two if you critically succeed:\n• They can’t move from where they are.\n• You deal damage to them equal to your primary weapon’s damage.\n• You move with them.","was":"Attack of Opportunity"},{"name":"Combat Training","text":"You ignore burden when equipping weapons. When you deal physical damage, you gain a bonus to your damage roll equal to your level.","was":"Combat Training"}],"subclasses":[{"name":"Merc","was":"Call of the Brave","tropes":"Militech veteran, Unification War vet, 6th Street — the one who gets braver as it gets worse.","note":"","description":"Play the Merc if you want to use the might of your enemies to fuel your own power.","spellcast_trait":"","foundation":[{"name":"Courage","text":"When you fail a roll with Fear, you gain a Hope.","was":"Courage"},{"name":"Battle Ritual","text":"Once per long rest, before you attempt something incredibly dangerous or face off against a foe who clearly outmatches you, describe what ritual you perform or preparations you make. When you do, clear 2 Stress and gain 2 Hope.","was":"Battle Ritual"}],"specialization":[{"name":"Rise to the Challenge","text":"You are vigilant in the face of mounting danger. While you have 2 or fewer Hit Points unmarked, you can roll a d20 as your Hope Die.","was":"Rise to the Challenge"}]},{"name":"Solo","was":"Call of the Slayer","tropes":"Heavily chromed solo, the Afterlife's best, famous on braindance for their fights.","note":"","description":"Play the Solo if you want to strike down adversaries with immense force.","spellcast_trait":"","foundation":[{"name":"Slayer","text":"You gain a pool of dice called Slayer Dice. On a roll with Hope, you can place a d6 on this card instead of gaining a Hope, adding the die to the pool. You can store a number of Slayer Dice equal to your Proficiency. When you make an attack roll or damage roll, you can spend any number of these Slayer Dice, rolling them and adding their result to the roll. At the end of each session, clear any unspent Slayer Dice on this card and gain a Hope per die cleared.","was":"Slayer"}],"specialization":[{"name":"Weapon Specialist","text":"You can wield multiple weapons with dangerous ease. When you succeed on an attack, you can spend a Hope to add one of the damage dice from your secondary weapon to the damage roll. Additionally, once per long rest when you roll your Slayer Dice, reroll any 1s.","was":"Weapon Specialist"}]}],"guide":{"traits":{"Movement":2,"Body":1,"Reflexes":0,"Empathy":1,"Cool":-1,"Intelligence":0},"primary":"Nodachi","secondary":"","armor":"Tactical Vest"}},{"name":"Netrunner","was":"Wizard","hf":false,"description":"Whether through an institution or individual study, those known as wizards acquire and hone immense magical power over years of learning using a variety of tools, including books, stones, potions, and herbs. Some wizards dedicate their lives to mastering a particular school of magic, while others learn from a wide variety of disciplines. Many wizards become wise and powerful figures in their communities, advising rulers, providing medicines and healing, and even leading war councils. While these mages all work toward the common goal of collecting magical knowledge, wizards often have the most conflict within their own ranks, as the acquisition, keeping, and sharing of powerful secrets is a topic of intense debate that has resulted in innumerable deaths.","domains":["Codex","Splendor"],"evasion":11,"hp":5,"class_items":"A cyberdeck older than you are, or a shard with one program you've never run","background_questions":["Who taught you to run the net, and what happened to them?","What's the one thing you've seen past the ICE that you've never told anyone?","Which corp's architecture do you know like your own apartment, and why?"],"connections":["What did I find in your files, and why haven't I mentioned it?","Why do you cover me when I'm jacked in?","What did you see me do in the net that you don't understand?"],"book_items":"A book you’re trying to translate or a tiny, harmless elemental pet","features":[{"name":"Not This Time","text":"to force an adversary within Far range to reroll an attack or damage roll.","was":"Not This Time"},{"name":"Prestidigitation","text":"You can perform harmless, subtle magical effects at will. For example, you can change an object’s color, create a smell, light a candle, cause a tiny object to float, illuminate a room, or repair a small object.","was":"Prestidigitation"},{"name":"Strange Patterns","text":"Choose a number between 1 and 12. When you roll that number on a Duality Die, gain a Hope or clear a Stress. You can change this number when you take a long rest.","was":"Strange Patterns"}],"subclasses":[{"name":"Netrunner (Exfil)","was":"School of Knowledge","tropes":"Data courier, corporate exfiltration specialist, the runner who always has one more program.","note":"","description":"Play the Exfil if you want a keen understanding of the world around you.","spellcast_trait":"Intelligence","foundation":[{"name":"Prepared","text":"Take an additional domain card of your level or lower from a domain you have access to.","was":"Prepared"},{"name":"Adept","text":"When you Utilize an Experience, you can mark a Stress instead of spending a Hope. If you do, double your Experience modifier for that roll.","was":"Adept"}],"specialization":[{"name":"Accomplished","text":"Take an additional domain card of your level or lower from a domain you have access to.","was":"Accomplished"},{"name":"Perfect Recall","text":"Once per rest, when you recall a domain card in your vault, you can reduce its Recall Cost by 1.","was":"Perfect Recall"}]},{"name":"Netrunner (Deletion)","was":"School of War","tropes":"Arasaka netsec, combat runner, Voodoo Boys-trained — an offensive hacker who fries people.","note":"","description":"Play the Deletion if you want to utilize trained magic for violence.","spellcast_trait":"Intelligence","foundation":[{"name":"Battlemage","text":"You’ve focused your studies on becoming an unconquerable force on the battlefield. Gain an additional Hit Point slot.","was":"Battlemage"},{"name":"Face Your Fear","text":"When you succeed with Fear on an attack roll, you deal an extra 1d10 electronic damage.","was":"Face Your Fear"}],"specialization":[{"name":"Conjure Shield","text":"You can maintain a protective barrier of magic. While you have at least 2 Hope, you add your Proficiency to your Evasion.","was":"Conjure Shield"},{"name":"Fueled by Fear","text":"The extra electronic damage from your “Face Your Fear” feature increases to 2d10.","was":"Fueled by Fear"}]}],"guide":{"traits":{"Movement":-1,"Body":0,"Reflexes":0,"Empathy":1,"Cool":1,"Intelligence":2},"primary":"Ashura Smart Sniper","secondary":"","armor":"Kevlar Vest"}},{"name":"Assassin","was":"Assassin","hf":true,"description":"Assassins are masters at inflicting deadly injuries with precise strikes. Unlike those who wield violence as only a means to an end, assassins approach death as a profession. Many members of this class believe theirs is a worthy, if not sacred, trade, and some join guilds to hone their craft, define their beliefs, and earn money. People from all walks of life hire assassins for their skills: powerful rulers looking to avoid all-out war, business leaders seeking to eliminate the competition, and even average people hoping to settle a grudge. Often, an assassin is the last resort for killing those previously believed to be unkillable. While some of these deadly professionals will destroy anyone in their path for the right reasons or the right price, others hold strict moral codes or personal rules that dictate their targets. Those who end up the target of an assassin should count themselves among the dead.","domains":["Blade","Midnight"],"evasion":12,"hp":5,"class_items":"A photo of a target you didn't take, or a single round you're saving","background_questions":["Who was the first, and what did they say to you?","Who's paying your bills now, and what do they think you owe them?","You let someone live once. Why, and where are they?"],"connections":["Why were you on the list, and why aren't you anymore?","What did I promise you, and do you believe me?","What do you think I do with the money?"],"book_items":"A list of names with several marked off or a rusted blade inscribed with an insignia","features":[{"name":"Deadly Determination","text":"Spend 3 Hope to clear 2 Stress.","was":"Deadly Determination"},{"name":"Marked for Death","text":"On a successful weapon attack, you can mark a Stress to make the target Marked for Death. When you deal damage to a target you’ve Marked for Death, add a number of d4s equal to your tier to the damage roll.\n\nYou can have only one adversary Marked for Death at a time. This condition lasts until you take a rest, the current adversary Marked for Death is defeated, or the GM spends a number of Fear equal to your tier to clear it.","was":"Marked for Death"},{"name":"Get In & Get Out","text":"Spend a Hope to ask the GM for a quick or inconspicuous way into or out of a place you can see. The next roll you make that acts on this information has advantage.","was":"Get In & Get Out"}],"subclasses":[{"name":"Hitman","was":"Executioners Guild","tropes":"Corporate wetwork, Tyger Claw enforcer, the first shot that ends it.","note":"","description":"Play the Hitman if you want to strike down your targets with lethal precision.","spellcast_trait":"Movement","foundation":[{"name":"First Strike","text":"The first time in a scene you succeed on an attack, you deal double damage.","was":"First Strike"},{"name":"Ambush","text":"Your “Marked for Death” feature uses d6s instead of d4s.","was":"Ambush"}],"specialization":[{"name":"Death Strike","text":"When you deal Severe damage to a creature, you can mark a Stress to force them to mark an additional Hit Point.","was":"Death Strike"},{"name":"Scorpion’s Poise","text":"You gain a +2 bonus to your Evasion against attacks made by a creature you’ve Marked for Death.","was":"Scorpion’s Poise"}]},{"name":"Chemist","was":"Poisoners Guild","tropes":"Toxin cook, Scav doctor, the no-chrome fixer's edge — poisons and afflictions.","note":"","description":"Play the Chemist if you want to debilitate your targets with punishing afflictions.","spellcast_trait":"Intelligence","foundation":[{"name":"Toxic Concoctions","text":"Mark a Stress to place 1d4+1 tokens on this card. When you make a successful weapon attack, you can spend a token to afflict the target with a poison. You know these poisons:\n• Ghost Petal: The target becomes temporarily Vulnerable. • Grave Spore: The target must also mark a Stress.\n• Leech Weed: You deal an extra 1d6 damage on this attack. When you take a long rest, clear all unspent tokens.","was":"Toxic Concoctions"}],"specialization":[{"name":"Poison Compendium","text":"You also know these poisons:\n• Midnight Vine: The target has disadvantage on attack rolls until it marks a Stress to clear this condition.\n• Gorgon Root: The target becomes temporarily Restrained.","was":"Poison Compendium"},{"name":"Twin Fang","text":"When you afflict a target Marked for Death with a poison you know, you can spend an additional token to also inflict the effect of a second poison you know.","was":"Twin Fang"}]}],"guide":null},{"name":"Brawler","was":"Brawler","hf":true,"description":"Experts in unarmed combat, brawlers hone their bodies into lethal weapons. Whether they learned from formal training, studied with a mentor, or picked up their skills one fight at a time, the process is always rigorous as brawlers develop their body and mind to work as one. Brawlers are valued for their power and versatility and typically join a party or a cause when the need or desire arises. Because a brawler’s body is their strongest weapon, they typically seek out new challenges or consistent sparring partners so they can maintain their skills and add new techniques to their repertoire. Though they might appear unassuming to those accustomed to foes who are armed to the teeth, brawlers often accomplish more with bare knuckles than an average soldier with a sword.","domains":["Valor","Bone"],"evasion":10,"hp":6,"class_items":"Hand wraps that have never been washed, or a championship belt from a fight club that's gone","background_questions":["Where did you learn to fight — a dojo, a cage, a corporate gym, a cell?","Who's the one you couldn't beat, and what did losing cost you?","What does your body not do anymore, and what did it take to make up for it?"],"connections":["Which of us swung first, and why are we still friends?","What did you bet on me, and did it pay?","Why do you patch me up instead of telling me to stop?"],"book_items":"Hand wraps from a mentor or a book about your secret hobby","features":[{"name":"Square Up","text":"Spend 3 Hope to intimidate a target within Close range, making them temporarily Vulnerable.","was":"Square Up"},{"name":"I Am the Weapon","text":"Your barehanded attacks are as strong as any blade. You have a primary weapon called Brawler’s Strike equipped while you have no other Active Weapons. It uses a trait of your choice, has Melee range, and deals d8+d6 physical damage using your Proficiency (both the d8 and d6 scale off your Proficiency). While this weapon is active, you gain a +1 bonus to your Evasion.","was":"I Am the Weapon"},{"name":"Combo Strike","text":"After rolling damage on a successful attack with a Melee weapon, you can mark a Stress to start a combo strike. When you do, roll your Combo Die and note the result, then continue rolling your Combo Die until the result of your latest roll is lower than the roll that preceded it. You deal extra damage equal to the total of all rolled Combo Die results on this attack. The results can’t be modified by any means. Your Combo Die starts as a d4. Once per tier, you can increase your Combo Die by one step as a level advancement option.","was":"Combo Strike"}],"subclasses":[{"name":"Juggernaut","was":"Juggernaut","tropes":"Full borg, Maelstrom heavy, the one built like a truck.","note":"","description":"Play the Juggernaut if you want to pulverize your opponents with crushing blows.","spellcast_trait":"","foundation":[{"name":"Rugged","text":"Gain a permanent +3 bonus to your Severe damage threshold.","was":"Rugged"},{"name":"Overwhelm","text":"When you succeed on an attack against a target, you can spend a Hope to throw the target within Close range or to force them to mark a Stress.","was":"Overwhelm"}],"specialization":[{"name":"Surrounded","text":"When you make an attack with a Melee weapon, you can spend any number of Hope to target an equal number of additional creatures within Melee range.","was":"Surrounded"},{"name":"Eye for an Eye","text":"Once per rest when an adversary within Melee range forces you to mark any number of Hit Points, you can mark a Stress to force them to mark the same number of Hit Points.","was":"Eye for an Eye"}]},{"name":"Martial Artist","was":"Martial Artist","tropes":"The Animals, a Tyger Claw dojo, the fight-club circuit.","note":"","description":"Play the Martial Artist if you want to use a variety of fighting styles to eliminate your foes.","spellcast_trait":"","foundation":[{"name":"Stance Fighter","text":"You can channel your inner resolve to shift into martial stances that grant you special benefits in combat. Take the Martial Stances sheet and choose two martial stances from Tier 1. Each time you level up your character, choose an additional stance from your tier or lower.","was":"Stance Fighter"}],"specialization":[{"name":"Keen Defenses","text":"When you’re targeted by an attack, you can spend a Focus to gain a bonus to your Evasion equal to your tier against the attack.","was":"Keen Defenses"},{"name":"Focus Cannon","text":"Spend a Focus to make an Empathy Roll against an adversary within Far range. On a success, deal d20+3 electronic damage using your Proficiency.","was":"Focus Cannon"}]}],"guide":null},{"name":"Netmerc","was":"Warlock","hf":true,"description":"Those who’ve traded their lives—or perhaps even their souls—to an otherworldly patron in exchange for incredible power are known as warlocks. Often, these mortals have reached a point of desperation that leads them to make this sacrifice: they hope to protect themselves or a loved one, aid their community, seek vengeance, increase their status, or otherwise further their ambitions. The powerful entities they entreat are as varied as the warlocks themselves: gods, spirits, demons, or other beings unknown to even the mortal who makes the pact. The entities each have their own sphere of influence that defines their otherworldly power, and those that collect souls are rarely known for their benevolence. Thus, a warlock’s power is defined by the relationship they maintain with their benefactor. Despite their terrifying magic, warlocks might find that someone or something else is pulling their strings.","domains":["Dread","Grace"],"evasion":11,"hp":5,"class_items":"A shard of code you didn't write, or the contact card of someone you've never seen","background_questions":["When did your patron first speak to you, and what did it ask for?","What have you already given up, and who noticed?","What does your patron tell you that your crew doesn't know?"],"connections":["What did you hear my patron say?","Why don't you ask about the favors?","What do you think I'll become, and will you stop me?"],"book_items":"A carving that symbolizes your patron or a ring you can’t remove","features":[{"name":"Patron’s Boon","text":"When you fail a roll, you can spend 3 Hope to reroll with advantage.","was":"Patron’s Boon"},{"name":"Patron’s Pact","text":"You have committed yourself to a supernatural entity—such as a god, fae, or demon—in exchange for power. Write their name on your character sheet, then work with your GM to determine their sphere of influence (such as Nature, Chaos, Wisdom, Mischief, Love, War, Justice, or Death). Before making an action roll that relates to your patron’s sphere of influence, you can spend a Favor to call upon their aid, rolling your Patron Die and adding its result to the total. Your Patron Die starts at a d6 and increases to a d8 at level 5.","was":"Patron’s Pact"},{"name":"Favor","text":"You start with 3 Favor. You can use a downtime move to show tribute to your patron. Describe how and gain Favor equal to your Netrun trait. Additionally, when you succeed on an action roll with Hope, you can choose to gain a Favor instead of a Hope.","was":"Favor"}],"subclasses":[{"name":"Netmerc (Blackwall)","was":"Pact of the Endless","tropes":"Voodoo Boys contractor — the patron is beyond the Blackwall, and you don't know it yet.","note":"","description":"Play the Blackwall if you want to stand strong against enemies and avoid death.","spellcast_trait":"Cool","foundation":[{"name":"Patron’s Mantle","text":"Spend a Favor to cloak yourself in a terrifying aspect of your Patron that lasts until you take Severe damage or the scene ends. While this effect is active, you gain a bonus to your damage thresholds equal to your tier and have advantage on action rolls to intimidate a target.","was":"Patron’s Mantle"},{"name":"Deathless Embrace","text":"Once per rest, spend any number of Favor to roll an equal number of Patron Dice. For each result of 4 or higher, clear a Hit Point.","was":"Deathless Embrace"}],"specialization":[{"name":"Harrowing Invocation","text":"When an adversary targets you or an ally within Very Close range with an attack, you can spend a Favor to give them disadvantage on the roll. If the adversary fails the roll, they must also mark a Stress.","was":"Harrowing Invocation"},{"name":"Damage Sink","text":"Once per rest, you can spend a Favor to halve incoming damage.","was":"Damage Sink"}]},{"name":"Netmerc (Bounty)","was":"Pact of the Wrathful","tropes":"NetWatch bounty runner, ICE hunter, the netmerc who destroys those who act against them.","note":"","description":"Play the Bounty if you want to destroy those who act against you.","spellcast_trait":"Cool","foundation":[{"name":"Patron’s Fury","text":"Spend a Favor to imbue your attacks with your Patron’s power until you deal Severe damage or the scene ends. When you roll damage while this effect is active, you also roll a number of Patron Dice equal to your tier and add their total to the damage dealt.","was":"Patron’s Fury"},{"name":"Deadly Vengeance","text":"When you mark any number of Hit Points from an attack, you can spend a Favor to roll an equal number of Patron Dice. For each result of 4 or higher, the attacker marks a Hit Point.","was":"Deadly Vengeance"}],"specialization":[{"name":"Menacing Reach","text":"Spend a Favor to increase the range of your primary weapon by one step (such as Melee to Very Close or Very Close to Close) to a maximum of Very Far range. This effect ends when you make a successful attack with that weapon.","was":"Menacing Reach"},{"name":"Diminish My Foes","text":"When you succeed with Hope on an action roll against a target, you can spend any number of Favor to force the target to mark an equal number of Stress.","was":"Diminish My Foes"}]}],"guide":null},{"name":"Witch","was":"Witch","hf":true,"description":"Witches are magical practitioners who commune with the forces of nature and entities from realms beyond. These spellcasters call forth power through craft, combining the tangible and ephemeral by casting spells, murmuring incantations, creating talismans, weaving illusions, and maintaining other personalized practices. They can protect their allies and harm their enemies by invoking powerful forces beyond themselves—supernatural beings such as ancestors, deities, or aspects of nature. Often, their magical knowledge is passed down from these entities or through many generations of practitioners who gather in small groups known as covens. Witches are frequently feared and misunderstood, as their methods can appear mysterious, strange, and even macabre to the uninitiated. Whichever path they walk, a witch treads the boundary between light and shadow without fear.","domains":["Sage","Dread"],"evasion":10,"hp":6,"class_items":"A chrome-lined case of tools, or a mirror you never look into","background_questions":["What do you make that nobody else in the city can, and who comes to you for it?","Who did you remake — including yourself — and what went wrong?","Something looks back at you from the mirror or the screen. What is it?"],"connections":["What did I fix for you, and what did I keep?","Why don't you look at my hands when I work?","Which one of us is the real one?"],"book_items":"A small, harmless pet or a scrying stone","features":[{"name":"Witch’s Charm","text":"When you or an ally within Far range fails an action roll, you can spend 3 Hope to change it into a success with Fear instead.","was":"Witch’s Charm"},{"name":"Hex","text":"Mark a Stress to temporarily Hex a target within Far range. While Hexed, the target gains a penalty to their damage rolls and Difficulty equal to your tier. The maximum number of creatures you can Hex at one time is equal to your Netrun trait.","was":"Hex"},{"name":"Commune","text":"Once per long rest during a moment of calm, you can commune with an ancestor, a deity, a spirit, or an otherworldly being. Ask them a question, then roll a number of d6s equal to your Netrun trait. Choose one of the results and reference the chart below for the effect.","was":"Commune"}],"subclasses":[{"name":"Ripperdoc","was":"Hedge","tropes":"Back-room ripper, street clinic, Kabuki chrome dealer — consumables and protective implants.","note":"","description":"Play the Hedge if you want to use your craft to empower yourself and your party.","spellcast_trait":"Intelligence","foundation":[{"name":"Herbal Remedies","text":"When you or an ally in the scene clears 1 or more Hit Points or Stress as the result of using a consumable, increase the number cleared by 1.","was":"Herbal Remedies"},{"name":"Enchanted Talisman","text":"Once per rest, you can imbue a small item with your protective essence. Spend any number of Hope to place an equal number of tokens on this card. When the person holding the talisman takes damage, spend a token to reduce the number of Hit Points they mark by one. Clear all tokens from this card when you take a rest.","was":"Enchanted Talisman"}],"specialization":[{"name":"Walk Between Worlds","text":"During a moment of calm, make a Quickhack Roll (13). Once per rest on a success, you can mark a Stress to step beyond the veil of death and converse with any nearby spirits. Place a number of tokens equal to your Netrun trait on this card and remove one each time a spirit answers a question. When the last token is removed or at the end of the scene, you return to the Mortal Realm in the same spot you left it.","was":"Walk Between Worlds"},{"name":"Vexing Malison","text":"You have advantage on attacks against Hexed creatures.","was":"Vexing Malison"}]},{"name":"Idol","was":"Moon","tropes":"Celebrity, influencer, braindance star — a glamour people would rather watch than shoot.","note":"","description":"Play the Moon if you want to embody celestial power to amplify your magic.","spellcast_trait":"Empathy","foundation":[{"name":"Night’s Glamour","text":"Make a Quickhack Roll (13) to Glamour yourself in a bewitching facade. While Glamoured, you have advantage on rolls that leverage your illusory appearance. Additionally, adversaries within Close range must mark a Stress to attack you.\n\nUnless you mark a Stress to maintain your Glamour, it drops when you mark a Hit Point or deal damage.","was":"Night’s Glamour"}],"specialization":[{"name":"Moonbeam","text":"Once per session, you can conjure a column of moonlight that illuminates the area within Close range until the end of the scene. While bathed in this moonlight, you and your allies gain a +1 bonus to Quickhack Rolls and can see through illusions.","was":"Moonbeam"},{"name":"Ire of Pale Light","text":"When a Hexed creature within Very Far range fails an attack, they must mark a Stress.","was":"Ire of Pale Light"}]}],"guide":null}],"domains":[{"name":"Arcana","was":"Arcana","description":"Arcana is the domain of innate and instinctual magic. Those who choose this path tap into the raw, enigmatic forces of the realms to manipulate both their own energy and the elements. Arcana offers wielders a volatile power, but it is incredibly potent when correctly channeled."},{"name":"Grace","was":"Grace","description":"Grace is the domain of charisma. Through rapturous storytelling, charming spells, or a shroud of lies, those who channel this power define the realities of their adversaries, bending perception to their will. Grace offers its wielders raw magnetism and mastery over language."},{"name":"Blade","was":"Blade","description":"Blade is the domain of weapon mastery. Whether by steel, bow, or perhaps a more specialized arm, those who follow this path have the skill to cut short the lives of others. Wielders of Blade dedicate themselves to achieving inexorable power over death."},{"name":"Midnight","was":"Midnight","description":"Midnight is the domain of shadows and secrecy. Whether by clever tricks, deft magic, or the cloak of night, those who channel these forces practice the art of obscurity and can uncover sequestered treasures. Midnight offers practitioners the power to control and create enigmas."},{"name":"Bone","was":"Bone","description":"Bone is the domain of tactics and the body. Practitioners of this domain have an uncanny control over their own physical abilities and an eye for predicting the behaviors of others in combat. Adherents to Bone gain an unparalleled understanding of bodies and their movements."},{"name":"Sage","was":"Sage","description":"Sage is the domain of the natural world. Those who walk this path tap into the unfettered power of the earth and its creatures to unleash raw magic. Sage grants its adherents the vitality of a blooming flower and the ferocity of a ravenous predator."},{"name":"Codex","was":"Codex","description":"Codex is the domain of intensive magical study. Those who seek magical knowledge turn to the equations of power recorded in books, written on scrolls, etched into walls, or tattooed on bodies. Codex offers a commanding and versatile understanding of magic to devotees who pursue knowledge beyond the boundaries of common wisdom."},{"name":"Splendor","was":"Splendor","description":"Splendor is the domain of life. Through this magic, followers gain the ability to heal and, to an extent, control death. Splendor offers its disciples the magnificent ability to both give and end life."},{"name":"Valor","was":"Valor","description":"Valor is the domain of protection. Whether through attack or defense, those who choose this discipline channel formidable strength to protect their allies in battle. Valor offers great power to those who raise their shields in defense of others."},{"name":"Dread","was":"Dread","description":"Some class options in this book use a domain called Dread. Dread is the domain of nightmares and fear. Those who choose this path can call forth monstrosities, enfeeble their foes, and channel terrifying magic to destroy their enemies. Dread grants its adherents power over forces most are too afraid to employ.\n\nDread domain cards are included in this expansion, and the Dread domain features can additionally be found in the appendix on page 180."}],"domain_cards":[{"name":"Rune Ward","was":"Rune Ward","domain":"Arcana","level":1,"type":"Spell","recall":0,"text":"You have a deeply personal trinket that can be infused with protective magic and held as a ward by you or an ally. Describe what it is and why it’s important to you. The ward’s holder can spend a Hope to reduce incoming damage by **1d8**.\n\nIf the Ward Die result is 8, the ward’s power ends after it reduces damage this turn. It can be recharged for free on your next rest.","converted":false},{"name":"Unleash Chaos","was":"Unleash Chaos","domain":"Arcana","level":1,"type":"Spell","recall":1,"text":"At the beginning of a session, place a number of tokens equal to your Netrun trait on this card.\n\nMake a **Quickhack Roll** against a target within Far range and spend any number of tokens to channel raw energy from within yourself to unleash against them. On a success, roll a number of **d10s** equal to the tokens you spent and deal that much electronic damage to the target. **Mark a Stress** to replenish this card with tokens (up to your Netrun trait).\n\nAt the end of each session, clear all unspent tokens.","converted":false},{"name":"Wall Walk","was":"Wall Walk","domain":"Arcana","level":1,"type":"Spell","recall":1,"text":"**Spend a Hope** to allow a creature you can touch to climb on walls and ceilings as easily as walking on the ground. This lasts until the end of the scene or you cast Wall Walk again.","converted":false},{"name":"Cinder Grasp","was":"Cinder Grasp","domain":"Arcana","level":2,"type":"Spell","recall":1,"text":"Make a **Quickhack Roll** against a target within Melee range. On a success, the target instantly bursts into flames, takes **1d20+3** electronic damage, and is temporarily lit *On Fire*.\n\nWhen a creature acts while *On Fire*, they must take an extra **2d6** electronic damage if they are still *On Fire* at the end of their action.","converted":false},{"name":"Floating Eye","was":"Floating Eye","domain":"Arcana","level":2,"type":"Spell","recall":0,"text":"**Spend a Hope** to create a single, small floating orb that you can move anywhere within Very Far range. While this spell is active, you can see through the orb as though you’re looking out from its position. You can transition between using your own senses and seeing through the orb freely. If the orb takes damage or moves out of range, the spell ends.","converted":false},{"name":"Counterspell","was":"Counterspell","domain":"Arcana","level":3,"type":"Spell","recall":2,"text":"You can interrupt a magical effect taking place by making a reaction roll using your Netrun trait. On a success, the effect stops and any consequences are avoided, and this card is placed in your vault.","converted":false},{"name":"Flight","was":"Flight","domain":"Arcana","level":3,"type":"Spell","recall":1,"text":"Make a **Quickhack Roll (15)**. On a success, place a number of tokens equal to your Movement on this card (minimum 1). When you make an action roll while flying, spend a token from this card. After the action that spends the last token is resolved, you descend to the ground directly below you.","converted":false},{"name":"Get Back Up","was":"Get Back Up","domain":"Blade","level":1,"type":"Ability","recall":1,"text":"When you take Severe damage, you can **mark a Stress** to reduce the severity by one threshold.","converted":false},{"name":"Not Good Enough","was":"Not Good Enough","domain":"Blade","level":1,"type":"Ability","recall":1,"text":"When you roll your damage dice, you can reroll any 1s or 2s.","converted":false},{"name":"Whirlwind","was":"Whirlwind","domain":"Blade","level":1,"type":"Ability","recall":0,"text":"When you make a successful attack against a target within Very Close range, you can **spend a Hope** to use the attack against all other targets within Very Close range. All additional adversaries you succeed against with this ability take half damage.","converted":false},{"name":"A Soldier’s Bond","was":"A Soldier’s Bond","domain":"Blade","level":2,"type":"Ability","recall":1,"text":"Once per long rest, when you compliment someone or ask them about something they’re good at, you can both gain 3 Hope.","converted":false},{"name":"Reckless","was":"Reckless","domain":"Blade","level":2,"type":"Ability","recall":1,"text":"**Mark a Stress** to gain advantage on an attack.","converted":false},{"name":"Scramble","was":"Scramble","domain":"Blade","level":3,"type":"Ability","recall":1,"text":"Once per rest, when a creature within Melee range would deal damage to you, you can avoid the attack and safely move out of Melee range of the enemy.","converted":false},{"name":"Versatile Fighter","was":"Versatile Fighter","domain":"Blade","level":3,"type":"Ability","recall":1,"text":"You can use a different character trait for an equipped weapon, rather than the trait the weapon calls for.\n\nWhen you deal damage, you can **mark a Stress** to use the maximum result of one of your damage dice instead of rolling it.","converted":false},{"name":"Deft Maneuvers","was":"Deft Maneuvers","domain":"Bone","level":1,"type":"Ability","recall":0,"text":"Once per rest, **mark a Stress** to sprint anywhere within Far range without making an Movement Roll to get there.\n\nIf you end this movement within Melee range of an adversary and immediately make an attack against them, gain a +1 bonus to the attack roll.","converted":false},{"name":"I See It Coming","was":"I See It Coming","domain":"Bone","level":1,"type":"Ability","recall":1,"text":"When you’re targeted by an attack made from beyond Melee range, you can **mark a Stress** to roll a **d4** and gain a bonus to your Evasion equal to the result against the attack.","converted":false},{"name":"Untouchable","was":"Untouchable","domain":"Bone","level":1,"type":"Ability","recall":1,"text":"Gain a bonus to your Evasion equal to half your Movement.","converted":false},{"name":"Ferocity","was":"Ferocity","domain":"Bone","level":2,"type":"Ability","recall":2,"text":"When you cause an adversary to mark 1 or more Hit Points, you can **spend 2 Hope** to increase your Evasion by the number of Hit Points they marked. This bonus lasts until after the next attack made against you.","converted":false},{"name":"Strategic Approach","was":"Strategic Approach","domain":"Bone","level":2,"type":"Ability","recall":1,"text":"After a long rest, place a number of tokens equal to your Intelligence on this card (minimum 1). The first time you move within Close range of an adversary and make an attack against them, you can spend one token to choose one of the following options:\n\n• You make the attack with advantage. • You clear a Stress on an ally within Melee range of the adversary. • You add a **d8** to your damage roll. When you take a long rest, clear all unspent tokens.","converted":false},{"name":"Brace","was":"Brace","domain":"Bone","level":3,"type":"Ability","recall":1,"text":"When you mark an Armor Slot to reduce incoming damage, you can **mark a Stress** to mark an additional Armor Slot.","converted":false},{"name":"Tactician","was":"Tactician","domain":"Bone","level":3,"type":"Ability","recall":1,"text":"When you Help an Ally, they can spend a Hope to add one of your Experiences to their roll alongside your advantage die.\n\nWhen making a Tag Team Roll, you can roll a **d20** as your Hope Die.","converted":false},{"name":"Ava.EXE","was":"Book Of Ava","domain":"Codex","level":1,"type":"Grimoire","recall":2,"text":"**Power Push:** Make a **Quickhack Roll** against a target within Melee range. On a success, they're knocked back to Far range and take **d10+2** electronic damage using your Proficiency.\n\n**Tava's Armor:** **Spend a Hope** to give a target you can touch a +1 bonus to their Armor Score until their next rest or you cast Tava's Armor again.\n\n**Brain Spike:** Make a **Quickhack Roll (12)** to trigger an electronic spike within Far range. If you use it as a weapon, make the Quickhack Roll against the target's Difficulty instead. On a success, deal **d6** physical damage using your Proficiency.","converted":true},{"name":"Illiat.EXE","was":"Book Of Illiat","domain":"Codex","level":1,"type":"Grimoire","recall":2,"text":"**Shadowban:** Make a **Quickhack Roll** against a target within Very Close range. On a success, they're *Disconnected* until they take damage or the GM spends a Fear on their turn to clear this condition.\n\n**DDOS:** Once per rest, **spend any number of Hope** and spam electronic attacks that strike a target of your choice within Close range. Roll a number of **d6s** equal to the Hope spent and deal that much electronic damage to the target.\n\n**Encrypted Connection: Spend a Hope** to open a line of mental communication with one target you can see. This connection lasts until your next rest or you cast Encrypted Connection again.","converted":true},{"name":"Tyfar.EXE","was":"Book Of Tyfar","domain":"Codex","level":1,"type":"Grimoire","recall":2,"text":"**Disrupt Cyberware:** Make a **Quickhack Roll** against up to three adversaries within Melee range. Targets you succeed against take **2d6** electronic damage and must mark a Stress as you superheat their augmentations.\n\n**Helper Drone:** You initialize a small drone the size of your hand within Far range.\n\n**Override Panopticon:** Make a **Quickhack Roll (13)** to disrupt all cameras within Very Close range. This heavily obscures this area and everything in it.","converted":true},{"name":"Sitil","was":"Book Of Sitil","domain":"Codex","level":2,"type":"Grimoire","recall":2,"text":"**Incognito Mode:** You shift your appearance and clothing to avoid recognition.\n\n**Parallela: Spend 2 Hope** to initialize this quickhack on yourself or an ally within Close range. The next time the target makes an attack, they can hit an additional target within range that their attack roll would succeed against. You can only hold this on one creature at a time.\n\n**Augment Reality:** Make a **Quickhack Roll (14)**. On a success, create a temporary visual illusion no larger than you within Close range that lasts for as long as you look at it. It holds up to scrutiny until an observer is within Melee range.","converted":true},{"name":"Vagras","was":"Book Of Vagras","domain":"Codex","level":2,"type":"Grimoire","recall":2,"text":"**Encrypted Lock:** Make a **Quickhack Roll (15)** on an object you're touching that can close. Once per rest on a success, you can lock the object so it can only be opened by entities of your choice. Someone with access to quickhacks and an hour of time to study the lock can break it.\n\n**Quick Exit:** When you have no adversaries within Melee range, make a **Quickhack Roll (13)**. On a success, **spend a Hope** to create a portal from where you are to a point within Far range you can see. It closes once a creature has passed through it.\n\n**Scan:** Make a **Quickhack Roll**. If there is anything electronically hidden within Close range, the roll would succeed against, it is revealed.","converted":true},{"name":"Book Of Korvax","was":"Book Of Korvax","domain":"Codex","level":3,"type":"Grimoire","recall":2,"text":"**Levitation:** Make a **Quickhack Roll** to temporarily lift a target you can see up into the air and move them within Close range of their original position.\n\n**Recant:** **Spend a Hope** to force a target within Melee range to make a Reaction Roll (15). On a failure, they forget the last minute of your conversation.\n\n**Rune Circle:** **Mark a Stress** to create a temporary magical circle on the ground where you stand. All adversaries within Melee range, or who enter Melee range, take **2d12+4** electronic damage and are knocked back to Very Close range.","converted":false},{"name":"Book Of Norai","was":"Book Of Norai","domain":"Codex","level":3,"type":"Grimoire","recall":2,"text":"**Mystic Tether:** Make a **Quickhack Roll** against a target within Far range. On a success, they’re temporarily *Restrained* and must mark a Stress. If you target a flying creature, this spell grounds and temporarily *Restrains* them.\n\n**Fireball:** Make a **Quickhack Roll** against a target within Very Far range. On a success, hurl a sphere of fire toward them that explodes on impact. The target and all creatures within Very Close range of them must make a Reaction Roll (13). Targets who fail take **d20+5** electronic damage using your Proficiency. Targets who succeed take half damage.","converted":false},{"name":"Blighting Strike","was":"Blighting Strike","domain":"Dread","level":1,"type":"Spell","recall":1,"text":"Make a Quickhack Roll against a target within Far range. On a success:\n• On a roll with Hope, deal d6+1 electronic damage using your Proficiency. On a roll with Fear, deal d10+1 electronic damage using your Proficiency.\n• The target’s next successful attack deals half damage.\nOn a failure, you must spend a Hope or mark a Stress.","converted":false},{"name":"Umbral Veil","was":"Umbral Veil","domain":"Dread","level":1,"type":"Spell","recall":1,"text":"Once per rest, you can mark a Stress to encase yourself in shadowy energy.\n\nWhen you do, place a number of tokens on this card equal to the number of Fear in the GM’s pool. After an attack roll is made against you, you can spend any number of tokens to give the result a −1 penalty per token spent.\n\nAt the end of the scene, clear all unspent tokens.","converted":false},{"name":"Voice Of Dread","was":"Voice Of Dread","domain":"Dread","level":1,"type":"Spell","recall":0,"text":"You can magically speak to a creature you can see, tormenting them with your words. Make a Quickhack Roll against them. On a success, they must mark a Stress and are frozen with terror, making them temporarily Restrained.","converted":false},{"name":"Hideous Retribution","was":"Hideous Retribution","domain":"Dread","level":2,"type":"Spell","recall":2,"text":"When an ally within Close range takes damage from a target you can see, you can make a reaction roll against the target using your Netrun trait. On a success, mark a Stress to deal d6 electronic damage using your Proficiency.","converted":false},{"name":"Siphon Essence","was":"Siphon Essence","domain":"Dread","level":2,"type":"Spell","recall":1,"text":"Make a Quickhack Roll against a target within Very Close range. Once per long rest on a success, the target takes d12+4 electronic damage using your Proficiency. On a success with Fear, you gain a +1 bonus to your Proficiency for this attack.\n\nYou clear a number of Hit Points equal to the number of Hit Points the target marked from this attack.","converted":false},{"name":"Shared Trauma","was":"Shared Trauma","domain":"Dread","level":3,"type":"Spell","recall":1,"text":"You can transfer suffering from one creature to another. Once per rest, mark any number of Hit Points on a willing creature within Melee range to clear an equal number of Hit Points on another willing creature within Melee range.","converted":false},{"name":"Terrify","was":"Terrify","domain":"Dread","level":3,"type":"Spell","recall":1,"text":"Make a Quickhack Roll against a target within Close range. On a success, the target marks 1d4 Stress, and you can make the target flee one range away from you (such as Very Close to Close or Close to Far). On a success with Fear, the target also becomes temporarily Vulnerable.","converted":false},{"name":"Deft Deceiver","was":"Deft Deceiver","domain":"Grace","level":1,"type":"Ability","recall":0,"text":"**Spend a Hope** to gain advantage on a roll to deceive or trick someone into believing a lie you tell them.","converted":false},{"name":"Enrapture","was":"Enrapture","domain":"Grace","level":1,"type":"Spell","recall":0,"text":"Make a **Quickhack Roll** against a target within Close range. On a success, they become temporarily *Enraptured*. While *Enraptured*, a target’s attention is fixed on you, narrowing their field of view and drowning out any sound but your voice. Once per rest on a success, you can **mark a Stress** to force the *Enraptured* target to mark a Stress as well.","converted":false},{"name":"Inspirational Words","was":"Inspirational Words","domain":"Grace","level":1,"type":"Ability","recall":1,"text":"Your speech is imbued with power. After a long rest, place a number of tokens on this card equal to your Cool. When you speak with an ally, you can spend a token from this card to give them one benefit from the following options:\n\n• Your ally clears a Stress. • Your ally clears a Hit Point. • Your ally gains a Hope. When you take a long rest, clear all unspent tokens.","converted":false},{"name":"Tell No Lies","was":"Tell No Lies","domain":"Grace","level":2,"type":"Spell","recall":1,"text":"Make a **Quickhack Roll** against a target within Very Close range. On a success, they can’t lie to you while they remain within Close range, but they are not compelled to speak. If you ask them a question and they refuse to answer, they must mark a Stress and the effect ends. The target is typically unaware this spell has been cast on them until it causes them to utter the truth.","converted":false},{"name":"Troublemaker","was":"Troublemaker","domain":"Grace","level":2,"type":"Ability","recall":2,"text":"When you taunt or provoke a target within Far range, make a **Cool Roll** against them. Once per rest on a success, roll a number of **d4s** equal to your Proficiency. The target must mark Stress equal to the highest result rolled.","converted":false},{"name":"Hypnotic Shimmer","was":"Hypnotic Shimmer","domain":"Grace","level":3,"type":"Spell","recall":1,"text":"Make a **Quickhack Roll** against all adversaries in front of you within Close range. Once per rest on a success, create an illusion of flashing colors and lights that temporarily *Stuns* targets you succeed against and forces them to mark a Stress. While *Stunned*, they can’t use reactions and can’t take any other actions until they clear this condition.","converted":false},{"name":"Invisibility","was":"Invisibility","domain":"Grace","level":3,"type":"Spell","recall":1,"text":"Make a **Quickhack Roll (10)**. On a success, **mark a Stress** and choose yourself or an ally within Melee range to become *Invisible*. An *Invisible* creature can’t be seen except through magical means and attack rolls against them are made with disadvantage. Place a number of tokens on this card equal to your Netrun trait. When the *Invisible* creature takes an action, spend a token from this card. After the action that spends the last token is resolved, the effect ends.\n\nYou can only hold Invisibility on one creature at a time.","converted":false},{"name":"Pick And Pull","was":"Pick And Pull","domain":"Midnight","level":1,"type":"Ability","recall":0,"text":"You have advantage on action rolls to pick nonmagical locks, disarm nonmagical traps, or steal items from a target (either through stealth or by force).","converted":false},{"name":"Rain Of Blades","was":"Rain Of Blades","domain":"Midnight","level":1,"type":"Spell","recall":1,"text":"**Spend a Hope** to make a **Quickhack Roll** and conjure throwing blades that strike out at all targets within Very Close range. Targets you succeed against take **d8+2** electronic damage using your Proficiency.\n\nIf a target you hit is *Vulnerable*, they take an extra **1d8** damage.","converted":false},{"name":"Uncanny Disguise","was":"Uncanny Disguise","domain":"Midnight","level":1,"type":"Spell","recall":0,"text":"When you have a few minutes to prepare, you can **mark a Stress** to don the facade of any humanoid you can picture clearly in your mind. While disguised, you have advantage on Cool Rolls to avoid scrutiny.\n\nPlace a number of tokens equal to your Netrun trait on this card. When you take an action while disguised, spend a token from this card. After the action that spends the last token is resolved, the disguise drops.","converted":false},{"name":"Midnight Spirit","was":"Midnight Spirit","domain":"Midnight","level":2,"type":"Spell","recall":1,"text":"**Spend a Hope** to summon a humanoid‑sized spirit that can move or carry things for you until your next rest.\n\nYou can also send it to attack an adversary. When you do, make a **Quickhack Roll** against a target within Very Far range. On a success, the spirit moves into Melee range with that target. Roll a number of **d6s** equal to your Netrun trait and deal that much electronic damage to the target. The spirit then dissipates. You can only have one spirit at a time.","converted":false},{"name":"Shadowbind","was":"Shadowbind","domain":"Midnight","level":2,"type":"Spell","recall":0,"text":"Make a **Quickhack Roll** against all adversaries within Very Close range. Targets you succeed against are temporarily *Restrained* as their shadow binds them in place.","converted":false},{"name":"Chokehold","was":"Chokehold","domain":"Midnight","level":3,"type":"Ability","recall":1,"text":"When you position yourself behind a creature who’s about your size, you can **mark a Stress** to pull them into a chokehold, making them temporarily *Vulnerable*.\n\nWhen a creature attacks a target who is *Vulnerable* in this way, they deal an extra **2d6** damage.","converted":false},{"name":"Veil Of Night","was":"Veil Of Night","domain":"Midnight","level":3,"type":"Spell","recall":1,"text":"Make a **Quickhack Roll (13)**. On a success, you can create a temporary curtain of darkness between two points within Far range. Only you can see through this darkness. You’re considered *Hidden* to adversaries on the other side of the veil, and you have advantage on attacks you make through the darkness. The veil remains until you cast another spell.","converted":false},{"name":"Gifted Tracker","was":"Gifted Tracker","domain":"Sage","level":1,"type":"Ability","recall":0,"text":"When you’re tracking a specific creature or group of creatures based on signs of their passage, you can **spend any number of Hope** and ask the GM that many questions from the following list.\n\n• What direction did they go? • How long ago did they pass through? • What were they doing in this location? • How many of them were here? When you encounter creatures you’ve tracked in this way, gain a +1 bonus to your Evasion against them.","converted":false},{"name":"Nature’s Tongue","was":"Nature’s Tongue","domain":"Sage","level":1,"type":"Ability","recall":0,"text":"You can speak the language of the natural world. When you want to speak to the plants and animals around you, make an **Empathy Roll (12)**. On a success, they’ll give you the information they know. On a roll with Fear, their knowledge might be limited or come at a cost.\n\nAdditionally, before you make a Quickhack Roll while within a natural environment, you can **spend a Hope** to gain a +2 bonus to the roll.","converted":false},{"name":"Vicious Entangle","was":"Vicious Entangle","domain":"Sage","level":1,"type":"Spell","recall":1,"text":"Make a **Quickhack Roll** against a target within Far range. On a success, roots and vines reach out from the ground, dealing **1d8+1** physical damage and temporarily *Restraining* the target.\n\nAdditionally on a success, you can **spend a Hope** to temporarily *Restrain* another adversary within Very Close range of your target.","converted":false},{"name":"Conjure Swarm","was":"Conjure Swarm","domain":"Sage","level":2,"type":"Spell","recall":1,"text":"**Tekaira Armored Beetles:** **Mark a Stress** to conjure armored beetles that encircle you. When you next take damage, reduce the severity by one threshold. You can **spend a Hope** to keep the beetles conjured after taking damage.\n\n**Fire Flies:** Make a **Quickhack Roll** against all adversaries within Close range. **Spend a Hope** to deal **2d8+3** electronic damage to targets you succeeded against.","converted":false},{"name":"Natural Familiar","was":"Natural Familiar","domain":"Sage","level":2,"type":"Spell","recall":1,"text":"**Spend a Hope** to summon a small nature spirit or forest critter to your side until your next rest, you cast Natural Familiar again, or the familiar is targeted by an attack. If you **spend an additional Hope**, you can summon a familiar that flies. You can communicate with them, make a **Quickhack Roll** to command them to perform simple tasks, and **mark a Stress** to see through their eyes.\n\nWhen you deal damage to an adversary within Melee range of your familiar, you add a **d6** to your damage roll.","converted":false},{"name":"Corrosive Projectile","was":"Corrosive Projectile","domain":"Sage","level":3,"type":"Spell","recall":1,"text":"Make a **Quickhack Roll** against a target within Far range. On a success, deal **d6+4** electronic damage using your Proficiency. Additionally, **mark 2 or more Stress** to make them permanently *Corroded*. While a target is *Corroded*, they gain a −1 penalty to their Difficulty for every 2 Stress you spent. This condition can stack.","converted":false},{"name":"Towering Stalk","was":"Towering Stalk","domain":"Sage","level":3,"type":"Spell","recall":1,"text":"Once per rest, you can conjure a thick, twisting stalk within Close range that can be easily climbed. Its height can grow up to Far range.\n\n**Mark a Stress** to use this spell as an attack. Make a **Quickhack Roll** against an adversary or group of adversaries within Close range. The erupting stalk lifts targets you succeed against into the air and drops them, dealing **d8** physical damage using your Proficiency.","converted":false},{"name":"Bolt Beacon","was":"Bolt Beacon","domain":"Splendor","level":1,"type":"Spell","recall":1,"text":"Make a **Quickhack Roll** against a target within Far range. On a success, **spend a Hope** to send a bolt of shimmering light toward them, dealing **d8+2** electronic damage using your Proficiency. The target becomes temporarily *Vulnerable* and glows brightly until this condition is cleared.","converted":false},{"name":"Mending Touch","was":"Mending Touch","domain":"Splendor","level":1,"type":"Spell","recall":1,"text":"You lay your hands upon a creature and channel healing magic to close their wounds. When you can take a few minutes to focus on the target you’re helping, you can **spend 2 Hope** to clear a Hit Point or a Stress on them.\n\nOnce per long rest, when you spend this healing time learning something new about them or revealing something about yourself, you can clear 2 Hit Points or 2 Stress on them instead.","converted":false},{"name":"Reassurance","was":"Reassurance","domain":"Splendor","level":1,"type":"Ability","recall":0,"text":"Once per rest, after an ally attempts an action roll but before the consequences take place, you can offer assistance or words of support. When you do, your ally can reroll their dice.","converted":false},{"name":"Final Words","was":"Final Words","domain":"Splendor","level":2,"type":"Spell","recall":1,"text":"You can infuse a corpse with a moment of life to speak with it. Make a **Quickhack Roll (13)**. On a success with Hope, the corpse answers up to three questions. On a success with Fear, the corpse answers one question. The corpse answers truthfully, but it can’t impart information it didn’t know in life. On a failure, or once the corpse has finished answering your questions, the body turns to dust.","converted":false},{"name":"Healing Hands","was":"Healing Hands","domain":"Splendor","level":2,"type":"Spell","recall":1,"text":"Make a **Quickhack Roll (13)** and target a creature other than yourself within Melee range. On a success, **mark a Stress** to clear 2 Hit Points or 2 Stress on the target. On a failure, **mark a Stress** to clear a Hit Point or a Stress on the target. You can’t heal the same target again until your next long rest.","converted":false},{"name":"Second Wind","was":"Second Wind","domain":"Splendor","level":3,"type":"Ability","recall":2,"text":"Once per rest, when you succeed on an attack against an adversary, you can clear 3 Stress or a Hit Point. On a success with Hope, you also clear 3 Stress or a Hit Point on an ally within Close range of you.","converted":false},{"name":"Voice Of Reason","was":"Voice Of Reason","domain":"Splendor","level":3,"type":"Ability","recall":1,"text":"You speak with an unmatched power and authority. You have advantage on action rolls to de‑escalate violent situations or convince someone to follow your lead.\n\nAdditionally, you’re emboldened in moments of duress. When all of your Stress slots are marked, you gain a +1 bonus to your Proficiency for damage rolls.","converted":false},{"name":"Concealed Body Armor","was":"Bare Bones","domain":"Valor","level":1,"type":"Ability","recall":0,"text":"When you choose not to equip armor, you have a base Armor Score of 3 + your Body and use the following as your base damage thresholds:\n\n• **Tier 1**: 9/19 • **Tier 2**: 11/24 • **Tier 3**: 13/31 • **Tier 4**: 15/38","converted":true},{"name":"Got You Covered","was":"I Am Your Shield","domain":"Valor","level":1,"type":"Ability","recall":1,"text":"When an ally within Very Close range would take damage, you can **mark a Stress** to stand in the way and make yourself the target of the attack instead. When you take damage from this attack, you can mark any number of Armor Slots.","converted":true},{"name":"Pulse","was":"Forceful Push","domain":"Valor","level":1,"type":"Ability","recall":0,"text":"Make an attack with your primary weapon against a target within Melee range. On a success, you deal damage and knock them back to Close range. On a success with Hope, add a **d6** to your damage roll.\n\nAdditionally, you can **spend a Hope** to make them temporarily *Vulnerable*.","converted":true},{"name":"Body Basher","was":"Body Basher","domain":"Valor","level":2,"type":"Ability","recall":1,"text":"You use the full force of your body in a fight. On a successful attack using a weapon with a Melee range, gain a bonus to your damage roll equal to your Body.","converted":false},{"name":"Bold Cool","was":"Bold Presence","domain":"Valor","level":2,"type":"Ability","recall":0,"text":"When you make a Cool Roll, you can **spend a Hope** to add your Body to the roll.\n\nAdditionally, once per rest when you would gain a condition, you can describe how your bold presence aids you in the situation and avoid gaining the condition.","converted":false},{"name":"Critical Inspiration","was":"Critical Inspiration","domain":"Valor","level":3,"type":"Ability","recall":1,"text":"Once per rest, when you critically succeed on an attack, all allies within Very Close range can clear a Stress or gain a Hope.","converted":false},{"name":"Lean On Me","was":"Lean On Me","domain":"Valor","level":3,"type":"Ability","recall":1,"text":"Once per long rest, when you console or inspire an ally who failed an action roll, you can both clear 2 Stress.","converted":false}],"communities":[{"name":"North Oaks","was":"Highborne","blurb":"Old money in the Westbrook hills — walled estates, private security, and a view of everything you'll never own.","text":"**Privilege:** You have advantage on rolls to consort with corpos, negotiate prices, or leverage your reputation to get what you want."},{"name":"Charter Hill","was":"Loreborne","blurb":"Westbrook's corporate families: gated condos, good schools, and a childhood spent learning which corp you belong to.","text":"**Well-Read:** You have advantage on rolls that involve the history, culture, or politics of a prominent person or place."},{"name":"Arasaka Waterfront","was":"Orderborne","blurb":"The one piece of Watson the corps kept — docks, warehouses, and a childhood inside the Arasaka compound.","text":"**Dedicated:** Record three sayings or values your upbringing instilled in you. Once per rest, when you describe how you're embodying one of these principles through your current action, you can roll a **d20** as your Hope Die."},{"name":"Southern Badlands","was":"Ridgeborne","blurb":"The Red Peaks and Jackson Plains past the city limits, where the road ends and Wraith country begins.","text":"**Steady:** You have advantage on rolls to traverse dangerous cliffs and ledges, navigate harsh environments, and use your survival knowledge."},{"name":"Wellsprings","was":"Seaborne","blurb":"Heywood's waterfront — boardwalks, ports, and the nicer apartments the middle managers come home to.","text":"**Know the Tide:** You can sense the ebb and flow of life. When you roll with Fear, place a token on your community card. You can hold a number of tokens equal to your level. Before you make an action roll, you can spend any number of these tokens to gain a +1 bonus to the roll for each token spent. At the end of each session, clear all unspent tokens."},{"name":"Kabuki","was":"Slyborne","blurb":"Watson's night markets: ripperdocs, braindance dens, back-alley editors, and everything the city sells after dark.","text":"**Scoundrel:** You have advantage on rolls to negotiate with criminals, detect lies, or find a safe place to hide."},{"name":"Little China","was":"Underborne","blurb":"Dense Watson blocks and the megabuildings — thousands of lives stacked in one tower, most of them yours.","text":"**Low-Light Living:** When you're in an area with low light or heavy shadow, you have advantage on rolls to hide, investigate, or perceive details within that area."},{"name":"The Badlands","was":"Wanderborne","blurb":"The desert outside the city — nomad convoys, dead farmland, wind farms, and the open road.","text":"**Nomadic Pack:** Add a Nomadic Pack to your inventory. Once per session, you can **spend a Hope** to reach into this pack and pull out a mundane item that's useful to your situation. Work with the GM to figure out what item you take out."},{"name":"Northside","was":"Wildborne","blurb":"Watson's derelict docks and factories, Maelstrom's ground, where you learned to move quietly through ruins.","text":"**Lightfoot:** Your movement is naturally silent. You have advantage on rolls to move without being heard."},{"name":"Eastern Badlands","was":"Duneborne","blurb":"Solar fields, oil fields, and Biotechnica's protein farms along the highway out of town.","text":"**Oasis:** During a short rest, you or an ally can reroll a die used for a downtime move and take the higher result."},{"name":"Pacifica","was":"Freeborne","blurb":"The resort the corps abandoned half-built — no NCPD, no corp security, and the people who stayed run it themselves.","text":"**Unbound:** Once per session when you roll with Fear, you can change it into a roll with Hope instead."},{"name":"Tycho, Lunar Colony","was":"Frostborne","blurb":"The Moon — cold, airless, corporate-run, and far enough away that Night City's problems take days to arrive.","text":"**Hardy:** When you take a rest, you clear a Hit Point."},{"name":"The Glen","was":"Hearthborne","blurb":"Heywood's civic heart — City Hall, the precinct, and the Valentinos' stronghold in the same few blocks, where family means neighbors.","text":"**Close-Knit:** Once per long rest, you can spend any number of Hope to grant an ally within Far range an equal number of Hope."},{"name":"Corpo Plaza","was":"Reborne","blurb":"The towers themselves — you had a corp, a badge, and a desk, and now you have none of them.","text":"**Found Family:** Once per rest, you can spend a Hope to use an ally’s Experience as if it were your own. When you do so, describe how your time with that ally prepared you for this moment.\n\nWhen you join a new community or rediscover your old one, you can permanently trade this card for that one instead."},{"name":"Rancho Coronado","was":"Warborne","blurb":"The planned suburb that went wrong — identical houses, 6th Street block parties, veterans of the Unification War.","text":"**Brave Face:** Once per session when you would be forced to mark a Stress, you can spend a Hope instead."}],"cyberware":[{"name":"Full Borg","was":"Clank","slot":"top","text":"Decide what corp built you, and to what purpose. At character creation, choose one of your Experiences that best aligns with this purpose and gain a permanent +1 bonus to it."},{"name":"Subdermal armor","was":"Drakona","slot":"top","text":"When you would take Severe damage, you can **mark a Stress** to mark 1 fewer Hit Points."},{"name":"Subdermal armor (alternate)","was":"Dwarf","slot":"top","text":"When you take Minor damage, you can **mark 2 Stress** instead of marking a Hit Point."},{"name":"Quick Reflexes","was":"Elf","slot":"top","text":"**Mark a Stress** to gain advantage on a reaction roll."},{"name":"Hot Hand","was":"Faerie","slot":"top","text":"Once per session, after you or a willing ally within Close range makes an action roll, you can **spend 3 Hope** to reroll the Duality Dice."},{"name":"Reinforced Tendons","was":"Faun","slot":"top","text":"You can leap anywhere within Close range as though you were using normal movement, allowing you to vault obstacles, jump across gaps, or scale barriers with ease."},{"name":"Charge","was":"Firbolg","slot":"top","text":"When you succeed on an Movement Roll to move from Far or Very Far range into Melee range with one or more targets, you can **mark a Stress** to deal **1d12** physical damage to all targets within Melee range."},{"name":"Commlink","was":"Fungril","slot":"top","text":"Make an **Empathy Roll (12)** to use your commlink array to speak with others of your network (gang/corp). On a success, you can communicate across any distance."},{"name":"Chitin Armor","was":"Galapa","slot":"top","text":"Gain a bonus to your damage thresholds equal to your Proficiency."},{"name":"Second Heart","was":"Giant","slot":"top","text":"Gain an additional Hit Point slot at character creation."},{"name":"Surefooted","was":"Goblin","slot":"top","text":"You ignore disadvantage on Movement Rolls."},{"name":"Life of the Party","was":"Halfling","slot":"top","text":"At the start of each session, everyone in your party gains a Hope."},{"name":"Iron Liver","was":"Human","slot":"top","text":"Gain an additional Stress slot at character creation."},{"name":"Fearless","was":"Infernis","slot":"top","text":"When you roll with Fear, you can **mark 2 Stress** to change it into a roll with Hope instead."},{"name":"Catlike Reflexes","was":"Katari","slot":"top","text":"When you make an Movement Roll, you can **spend 2 Hope** to reroll your Hope Die."},{"name":"Sturdy","was":"Orc","slot":"top","text":"When you have 1 Hit Point remaining, attacks against you have disadvantage."},{"name":"Amphibious","was":"Ribbet","slot":"top","text":"You can breathe and move naturally underwater."},{"name":"Natural Climber","was":"Simiah","slot":"top","text":"You have advantage on Movement Rolls that involve balancing and climbing."},{"name":"Good Vibes","was":"Aetheris","slot":"top","text":"Once per long rest when an ally within Close range rolls with Fear, you can change it into a roll with Hope instead."},{"name":"Carapace","was":"Earthkin","slot":"top","text":"Gain a permanent +1 bonus to your Armor Score and damage thresholds at character creation."},{"name":"Fireproof Skin","was":"Emberkin","slot":"top","text":"You are immune to damage from magical or mundane flame."},{"name":"Pneumatic Thrusters","was":"Skykin","slot":"top","text":"Mark a Stress to conjure a gust of wind that carries you or a Very Close ally up to Very Far range. Additionally, you can always control the speed at which you fall."},{"name":"Micro-manipulators","was":"Gnome","slot":"top","text":"When you make a Reflexes Roll, you can spend 2 Hope to reroll your Hope Die."},{"name":"Efficient","was":"Clank","slot":"bottom","text":"When you take a short rest, you can choose a long rest move instead of a short rest move."},{"name":"Elemental Breath","was":"Drakona","slot":"bottom","text":"Choose an element for your breath (such as electricity, fire, or ice). You can use this breath against a target or group of targets within Very Close range, treating it as an Empathy weapon that deals **d8** physical damage using your Proficiency."},{"name":"Increased Fortitude","was":"Dwarf","slot":"bottom","text":"**Spend 3 Hope** to halve incoming physical damage."},{"name":"Hyperfocus","was":"Elf","slot":"bottom","text":"During a rest, you can drop into a trance to choose an additional downtime move."},{"name":"Flying","was":"Faerie","slot":"bottom","text":"You can fly. While flying, you can **mark a Stress** after an adversary makes an attack against you to gain a +2 bonus to your Evasion against that attack."},{"name":"Kick","was":"Faun","slot":"bottom","text":"When you succeed on an attack against a target within Melee range, you can **mark a Stress** to kick yourself off them, dealing an extra **2d6** damage and knocking back either yourself or the target to Very Close range."},{"name":"Unshakable","was":"Firbolg","slot":"bottom","text":"When you would mark a Stress, roll a **d6**. On a result of 6, don’t mark it."},{"name":"BD Extraction","was":"Fungril","slot":"bottom","text":"While touching a corpse that died recently, you can **mark a Stress** to extract one memory from the corpse related to a specific emotion or sensation of your choice."},{"name":"Retract (requires Carapace)","was":"Galapa","slot":"bottom","text":"**Mark a Stress** to retract into your carapace. While in your carapace, you have resistance to physical damage, you have disadvantage on action rolls, and you can’t move."},{"name":"Extendable Limbs","was":"Giant","slot":"bottom","text":"Treat any weapon, ability, spell, or other feature that has a Melee range as though it has a Very Close range instead."},{"name":"Danger Sense","was":"Goblin","slot":"bottom","text":"Once per rest, **mark a Stress** to force an adversary to reroll an attack against you or an ally within Very Close range."},{"name":"Internal Compass","was":"Halfling","slot":"bottom","text":"When you roll a 1 on your Hope Die, you can reroll it."},{"name":"Adaptability","was":"Human","slot":"bottom","text":"When you fail a roll that utilized one of your Experiences, you can **mark a Stress** to reroll."},{"name":"HUD Scanner","was":"Infernis","slot":"bottom","text":"You have advantage on rolls to intimidate hostile creatures."},{"name":"Mantis Blades","was":"Katari","slot":"bottom","text":"Make an **Movement Roll** to scratch a target within Melee range. On a success, they become temporarily *Vulnerable*."},{"name":"Tusks","was":"Orc","slot":"bottom","text":"When you succeed on an attack against a target within Melee range, you can **spend a Hope** to gore the target with your tusks, dealing an extra **1d6** damage."},{"name":"Ballistic Grapple","was":"Ribbet","slot":"bottom","text":"You can use your long tongue to grab onto things within Close range. **Mark a Stress** to use your tongue as a Reflexes Close weapon that deals **d12** physical damage using your Proficiency."},{"name":"Nimble","was":"Simiah","slot":"bottom","text":"Gain a permanent +1 bonus to your Evasion at character creation."},{"name":"Flying (alt)","was":"Aetheris","slot":"bottom","text":"You have wings that allow you to fly. Once per scene while flying, you can spend a Hope instead of marking an Armor Slot."},{"name":"Focus Gravity","was":"Earthkin","slot":"bottom","text":"While you’re touching the ground, you can’t be lifted or moved against your will."},{"name":"Ignite","was":"Emberkin","slot":"bottom","text":"Mark a Stress to wreathe your primary weapon in flame until the end of the scene. While the weapon is ablaze, it gives off a bright light, and you gain a 1d6 bonus to damage rolls with that weapon."},{"name":"Active Alert","was":"Skykin","slot":"bottom","text":"Spend 2 Hope to grant you or an ally within Melee range a +1 bonus to Evasion until you take Severe damage or you use this feature again."},{"name":"Filtration System","was":"Tidekin","slot":"bottom","text":"Once per rest when you have access to a small amount of water, you can mark a Stress to clear a Hit Point on yourself or an ally within Very Close range."},{"name":"Blur","was":"Gnome","slot":"bottom","text":"Once per scene, you can teleport to another point you can see within Far range."}],"equipment":{"weapons":[{"name":"Machete","was":"Broadsword","category":"Primary","trait":"Movement","range":"Melee","damage":"d8 phy","damage_type":"Physical","burden":"One-Handed","feature":"Reliable: +1 to attack rolls"},{"name":"Nodachi","was":"Longsword","category":"Primary","trait":"Movement","range":"Melee","damage":"d8+3 phy","damage_type":"Physical","burden":"Two-Handed","feature":""},{"name":"Fire Axe","was":"Battleaxe","category":"Primary","trait":"Body","range":"Melee","damage":"d10+3 phy","damage_type":"Physical","burden":"Two-Handed","feature":""},{"name":"Chainsword","was":"Greatsword","category":"Primary","trait":"Body","range":"Melee","damage":"d10+3 phy","damage_type":"Physical","burden":"Two-Handed","feature":""},{"name":"Crowbar","was":"Mace","category":"Primary","trait":"Body","range":"Melee","damage":"d8+1 phy","damage_type":"Physical","burden":"One-Handed","feature":""},{"name":"Sledgehammer","was":"Warhammer","category":"Primary","trait":"Body","range":"Melee","damage":"d12+3 phy","damage_type":"Physical","burden":"Two-Handed","feature":"Heavy: −1 to Evasion"},{"name":"Combat Knife","was":"Dagger","category":"Primary","trait":"Reflexes","range":"Melee","damage":"d8+1 phy","damage_type":"Physical","burden":"One-Handed","feature":""},{"name":"Riot Staff","was":"Quarterstaff","category":"Primary","trait":"Empathy","range":"Melee","damage":"d10+3 phy","damage_type":"Physical","burden":"Two-Handed","feature":""},{"name":"Kukri","was":"Cutlass","category":"Primary","trait":"Cool","range":"Melee","damage":"d8+1 phy","damage_type":"Physical","burden":"One-Handed","feature":""},{"name":"Nanoblade","was":"Rapier","category":"Primary","trait":"Cool","range":"Melee","damage":"d8 phy","damage_type":"Physical","burden":"One-Handed","feature":"Quick: When you make an attack, you can mark a Stress to target another creature within range."},{"name":"Industrial Pike","was":"Halberd","category":"Primary","trait":"Body","range":"Very Close","damage":"d10+2 phy","damage_type":"Physical","burden":"Two-Handed","feature":"Cumbersome: −1 to Reflexes"},{"name":"Cattle Prod","was":"Spear","category":"Primary","trait":"Reflexes","range":"Very Close","damage":"d10+2 phy","damage_type":"Physical","burden":"Two-Handed","feature":"Cumbersome: −1 to Reflexes"},{"name":"Lexington SMG","was":"Shortbow","category":"Primary","trait":"Movement","range":"Far","damage":"d6+3 phy","damage_type":"Physical","burden":"Two-Handed","feature":""},{"name":"Unity Pistol","was":"Crossbow","category":"Primary","trait":"Reflexes","range":"Far","damage":"d6+1 phy","damage_type":"Physical","burden":"One-Handed","feature":""},{"name":"Ajax Rifle","was":"Longbow","category":"Primary","trait":"Movement","range":"Very Far","damage":"d8+3 phy","damage_type":"Physical","burden":"Two-Handed","feature":"Cumbersome: −1 to Reflexes"},{"name":"Shock Gauntlets","was":"Arcane Gauntlets","category":"Primary","trait":"Body","range":"Melee","damage":"d10+3 mag","damage_type":"Magic","burden":"Two-Handed","feature":""},{"name":"Arc Hatchet","was":"Hallowed Axe","category":"Primary","trait":"Body","range":"Melee","damage":"d8+1 mag","damage_type":"Magic","burden":"One-Handed","feature":""},{"name":"Satara Tech Shotgun","was":"Glowing Rings","category":"Primary","trait":"Movement","range":"Very Close","damage":"d10+1 mag","damage_type":"Magic","burden":"Two-Handed","feature":""},{"name":"Zap Glove","was":"Hand Runes","category":"Primary","trait":"Empathy","range":"Very Close","damage":"d10 mag","damage_type":"Magic","burden":"One-Handed","feature":""},{"name":"Monowire","was":"Returning Blade","category":"Primary","trait":"Reflexes","range":"Close","damage":"d8 mag","damage_type":"Magic","burden":"One-Handed","feature":""},{"name":"Shock Baton","was":"Shortstaff","category":"Primary","trait":"Empathy","range":"Close","damage":"d8+1 mag","damage_type":"Magic","burden":"One-Handed","feature":""},{"name":"Widow Maker Tech Rifle","was":"Dualstaff","category":"Primary","trait":"Empathy","range":"Far","damage":"d6+3 mag","damage_type":"Magic","burden":"Two-Handed","feature":""},{"name":"Shingen Smart SMG","was":"Scepter","category":"Primary","trait":"Cool","range":"Far","damage":"d6 mag","damage_type":"Magic","burden":"Two-Handed","feature":"Versatile: This weapon can also be used with these statistics—Cool, Melee, d8."},{"name":"Omaha Tech Pistol","was":"Wand","category":"Primary","trait":"Intelligence","range":"Far","damage":"d6+1 mag","damage_type":"Magic","burden":"One-Handed","feature":""},{"name":"Ashura Smart Sniper","was":"Greatstaff","category":"Primary","trait":"Intelligence","range":"Very Far","damage":"d6 mag","damage_type":"Magic","burden":"Two-Handed","feature":"Powerful: On a successful attack, roll an additional damage die and discard the lowest result."},{"name":"Light-Frame Wheelchair","was":"Light-Frame Wheelchair","category":"Primary","trait":"Movement","range":"Melee","damage":"d8 phy","damage_type":"Physical","burden":"One-Handed","feature":"Quick: When you make an attack, you can mark a Stress to target another creature within range."},{"name":"Heavy-Frame Wheelchair","was":"Heavy-Frame Wheelchair","category":"Primary","trait":"Body","range":"Melee","damage":"d12+3 phy","damage_type":"Physical","burden":"Two-Handed","feature":"Heavy: −1 to Evasion"},{"name":"Arcane-Frame Wheelchair","was":"Arcane-Frame Wheelchair","category":"Primary","trait":"Quickhack","range":"Far","damage":"d6 mag","damage_type":"Magic","burden":"One-Handed","feature":"Reliable: +1 to attack rolls"},{"name":"Tanto","was":"Shortsword","category":"Secondary","trait":"Movement","range":"Melee","damage":"d8 phy","damage_type":"Physical","burden":"One-Handed","feature":"Paired: +2 to primary weapon damage to targets within Melee range"},{"name":"Ballistic Shield","was":"Round Shield","category":"Secondary","trait":"Body","range":"Melee","damage":"d4 phy","damage_type":"Physical","burden":"One-Handed","feature":"Protective: +1 to Armor Score"},{"name":"Riot Shield","was":"Tower Shield","category":"Secondary","trait":"Body","range":"Melee","damage":"d6 phy","damage_type":"Physical","burden":"One-Handed","feature":"Barrier: +2 to Armor Score; −1 to Evasion"},{"name":"Switchblade","was":"Small Dagger","category":"Secondary","trait":"Reflexes","range":"Melee","damage":"d8 phy","damage_type":"Physical","burden":"One-Handed","feature":"Paired: +2 to primary weapon damage to targets within Melee range"},{"name":"Shock Whip","was":"Whip","category":"Secondary","trait":"Cool","range":"Very Close","damage":"d6 phy","damage_type":"Physical","burden":"One-Handed","feature":"Startling: Mark a Stress to crack the whip and force all adversaries within Melee range back to Close range."},{"name":"Grapple Gun","was":"Grappler","category":"Secondary","trait":"Reflexes","range":"Close","damage":"d6 phy","damage_type":"Physical","burden":"One-Handed","feature":"Hooked: On a successful attack, you can pull the target into Melee range."},{"name":"Liberty Holdout Pistol","was":"Hand Crossbow","category":"Secondary","trait":"Reflexes","range":"Far","damage":"d6+1 phy","damage_type":"Physical","burden":"One-Handed","feature":""},{"name":"Katana","was":"Katana","category":"Primary","trait":"Movement","range":"Melee","damage":"d10+3 phy","damage_type":"Physical","burden":"Two-Handed","feature":"Quick: When you make an attack, you can mark a Stress to target another creature within range."},{"name":"Brass Knuckles","was":"Brass Knuckles","category":"Primary","trait":"Body","range":"Melee","damage":"d8+1 phy","damage_type":"Physical","burden":"One-Handed","feature":""},{"name":"Cleaver","was":"Scimitar","category":"Primary","trait":"Cool","range":"Melee","damage":"d8+1 phy","damage_type":"Physical","burden":"One-Handed","feature":""},{"name":"Ice Pick","was":"Twisted Dagger","category":"Primary","trait":"Intelligence","range":"Melee","damage":"d8 phy","damage_type":"Physical","burden":"One-Handed","feature":"Piercing: Damage dealt with this weapon treats the target’s Major threshold as having a −2 penalty."},{"name":"Nano-Whip","was":"Whipsword","category":"Primary","trait":"Reflexes","range":"Very Close","damage":"d8 phy","damage_type":"Physical","burden":"One-Handed","feature":"Versatile: This weapon can also be used with these statistics—Reflexes, Melee, d10."},{"name":"Weighted Chain","was":"Rope Dart","category":"Primary","trait":"Empathy","range":"Close","damage":"d6+1 phy","damage_type":"Physical","burden":"Two-Handed","feature":""},{"name":"Thermal Katana","was":"Brightsword","category":"Primary","trait":"Body","range":"Melee","damage":"d10+3 mag","damage_type":"Magic","burden":"Two-Handed","feature":""},{"name":"Phase Knife","was":"Shadowblade","category":"Primary","trait":"Cool","range":"Melee","damage":"d8 phy/mag","damage_type":"Magic","burden":"One-Handed","feature":"Otherworldly: On a successful attack, you can deal physical or electronic damage."},{"name":"Ricochet Disc","was":"Enchanted Chakram","category":"Primary","trait":"Reflexes","range":"Close","damage":"d6+1 mag","damage_type":"Magic","burden":"One-Handed","feature":"Ricochet: When you throw this weapon, it returns to your hand. When you make an attack, you can mark a Stress to target another creature within Very Close range of the first target with that attack."},{"name":"Shock Knife","was":"Casting Dagger","category":"Primary","trait":"Empathy","range":"Close","damage":"d6 mag","damage_type":"Magic","burden":"One-Handed","feature":"Versatile: This weapon can also be used with these statistics—Empathy, Melee, d8."},{"name":"Chao Smart Pistol","was":"Runelock Pistol","category":"Primary","trait":"Intelligence","range":"Far","damage":"d6+3 mag","damage_type":"Magic","burden":"One-Handed","feature":"Reloading: After you make an attack, roll a d6. On a result of 1, you must mark a Stress to reload this weapon before you can fire it again."},{"name":"Nekomata Tech Sniper","was":"Arcane Rifle","category":"Primary","trait":"Movement","range":"Very Far","damage":"d10+3 mag","damage_type":"Magic","burden":"Two-Handed","feature":"Aimed: Your attack has disadvantage if the target is within Very Close range of you or within Melee range of one of your allies. You can mark a Stress to ignore this penalty."},{"name":"Hatchet","was":"Hatchet","category":"Secondary","trait":"Movement","range":"Melee","damage":"d8 phy","damage_type":"Physical","burden":"One-Handed","feature":"Follow-Up: On a successful attack with your primary weapon within Melee range, you can mark a Stress to gain a +1 bonus to your Proficiency for this attack."},{"name":"Offhand Brass Knuckles","was":"Offhand Brass Knuckles","category":"Secondary","trait":"Body","range":"Melee","damage":"d8 phy","damage_type":"Physical","burden":"One-Handed","feature":"Paired: +2 to primary weapon damage to targets within Melee range"},{"name":"Throwing Knives","was":"Throwing Knives","category":"Secondary","trait":"Reflexes","range":"Melee","damage":"d8 phy","damage_type":"Physical","burden":"One-Handed","feature":"Stockpiled: You can throw this weapon within Close range by making an attack roll using Reflexes. You don’t have to retrieve it, as you always have another on hand."},{"name":"Armored Coat","was":"Fighting Cloak","category":"Secondary","trait":"Cool","range":"Melee","damage":"d4 phy","damage_type":"Physical","burden":"One-Handed","feature":"Padded: +2 to damage thresholds"},{"name":"Holo-Shield","was":"Rune Shield","category":"Secondary","trait":"Intelligence","range":"Melee","damage":"d4 mag","damage_type":"Magic","burden":"One-Handed","feature":"Protective: +1 to Armor Score"},{"name":"Targeting Module","was":"Focus Runes","category":"Secondary","trait":"Empathy","range":"Very Close","damage":"d6 mag","damage_type":"Magic","burden":"One-Handed","feature":"Focused: +1 to primary weapon damage to targets within Very Close range"}],"armor":[{"name":"Ballistic Jacket","was":"Gambeson Armor","major":5,"severe":11,"score":3,"feature":"Flexible: +1 to Evasion"},{"name":"Kevlar Vest","was":"Leather Armor","major":6,"severe":13,"score":3,"feature":""},{"name":"Tactical Vest","was":"Chainmail Armor","major":7,"severe":15,"score":4,"feature":"Heavy: −1 to Evasion"},{"name":"Riot Armor","was":"Full Plate Armor","major":8,"severe":17,"score":4,"feature":"Very Heavy: −2 to Evasion; −1 to Movement"},{"name":"Netrunner Suit","was":"Mage Robes","major":4,"severe":10,"score":2,"feature":"Enchanted: Gain a bonus to your damage thresholds equal to your Netrun trait."},{"name":"Armored Trench Coat","was":"Brigandine Armor","major":6,"severe":12,"score":3,"feature":"Lined: Mark a Stress to negate Minor damage."},{"name":"Plated Vest","was":"Scale Mail Armor","major":7,"severe":14,"score":3,"feature":"Cumbersome: −1 to Reflexes"},{"name":"Heavy Plate Carrier","was":"Banded Armor","major":8,"severe":16,"score":4,"feature":"Bulky: −1 to Evasion; when you take Severe damage, you must mark a Stress."}]},"steps":[{"n":1,"title":"Choose a class and subclass","text":"#### CHARACTER SHEET, CHARACTER GUIDE, AND SUBCLASS CARD\n\nOnce you’ve chosen your class, grab the corresponding character sheet and character guide, then decide on a subclass and take that subclass’s foundation card. The character sheet is for recording your character’s details, so make sure you have paper or digital copies available. If you’re filling out a hard copy, use a pencil, as you’ll change details on the sheet as your character levels up. You can find a completed character sheet in the upcoming “Example Character” section.\n\nCustomized character sheets and character guides are available for download at www.daggerheart.com. A general character sheet is also available in the appendix.\n\n#### RECORD YOUR LEVEL\n\nCharacters in Daggerheart start at level 1. Record your level in the appropriate section at the top of your character sheet, and make sure to change it every time you level up. We recommend starting your campaign at level 1, but a more experienced table can start at a higher level if the GM and players prefer.\n\n#### RECORD CHARACTER DETAILS\n\nAt any point in the character creation process, feel free to fill out your character’s name and pronouns on the top line, as well as the Character Description details on the character guide. For some people, their character’s name and appearance come first, and for others, it takes time to discover what’s right. As long as you have a name and description by the time your table is finished with character creation, it doesn’t matter what order you complete these steps in.\n\n#### CLASS FEATURE\n\nEvery class begins with a unique **class feature** (or several). You don’t have to write these features down—they’re provided at the bottom left of each class’s character sheet. These are features available only to your class. If your class feature asks you to make a selection during character creation, make it before your first session."},{"n":2,"title":"Choose Your Heritage","text":"Next, choose your character’s **heritage**. This aspect of your character includes two elements—their ancestry and their community.\n\n#### CHOOSE YOUR ANCESTRY\n\nA character’s **ancestry** reflects their lineage, impacting their physicality and granting them two unique features. Take the card for one of the following ancestries, then write its name in the Heritage field of your character sheet:\n\n*You can learn more about these ancestries in the upcoming “Ancestry” section.*\n\n#### CHOOSE YOUR COMMUNITY\n\nNext, you’ll choose a **community** from the available community cards. Your character’s community is influenced by its physical location, values, and goals. It informs the culture your character grew up in and might play a part in the way they look, act, or approach situations. Each community comes with a feature that you can utilize during the game. Take the card for one of the following communities, then write its name in the Heritage field of your character sheet:\n\n> | North Oaks | Southern Badlands | Little China |\n> |---|---|---|\n> | Charter Hill | Wellsprings | The Badlands |\n> | Arasaka Waterfront | Kabuki | Northside |\n\n*You can learn more about these communities in the upcoming “Community” section.*\n\nLANGUAGES In Daggerheart, you’re not asked to pick specific languages for your character. This game assumes that everyone speaks a common language (it’s up to you whether that’s through mundane or magical means) and that sign language is widely understood across cultures and communities. If you’d like to have specific regional languages in your campaign, discuss options with your table."},{"n":3,"title":"Assign Character Traits","text":"Next, you’ll assign values to the **character traits** on your character sheet. These values reflect your natural or trained ability in each of the core six stats: Movement, Body, Reflexes, Empathy, Cool, and Intelligence. Each trait lists verbs (such as Sprint, Leap, and Maneuver) that show the types of actions a character might perform that involve that trait. These examples are just inspiration—they shouldn’t limit how each trait can be used.\n\nAGILITY: Sprint, Leap, Maneuver\n\nA high Movement means you’re fast on your feet, nimble on difficult terrain, and quick to react to danger. You’ll make an Movement Roll to scurry up a rope, sprint to cover, or bound from rooftop to rooftop.\n\nSTRENGTH: Lift, Smash, Grapple\n\nA high Body means you’re better at feats that test your physical prowess and stamina. You’ll make a Body Roll to break through a door, lift heavy objects, or hold your ground against a charging foe.\n\nFINESSE: Control, Hide, Tinker\n\nA high Reflexes means you’re skilled at tasks that require accuracy, stealth, or the utmost control. You’ll make a Reflexes Roll to use fine tools, escape notice, or strike with precision.\n\nINSTINCT: Perceive, Sense, Navigate\n\nA high Empathy means you have a keen sense of your surroundings and a natural intuition. You’ll make an Empathy Roll to sense danger, notice details in the world around you, or track an elusive foe.\n\nPRESENCE: Charm, Perform, Deceive\n\nA high Cool means you have a strong force of personality and a facility for social situations. You’ll make a Cool Roll to plead your case, intimidate a foe, or capture the attention of a crowd.\n\nKNOWLEDGE: Recall, Analyze, Comprehend\n\nA high Intelligence means you know information others don’t and understand how to apply your mind through deduction and inference. You’ll make a Intelligence Roll to interpret facts, see the patterns clearly, or remember important information.\n\n#### DISTRIBUTE TRAIT MODIFIERS\n\nTrait **modifiers** are the values associated with each trait. When you make an action roll using one of these traits, that trait’s modifier is added to the roll to determine the final result (or subtracted from the roll for a negative modifier).\n\nDistribute the following starting modifiers across your character’s traits in any order you wish: +2, +1, +1, 0, 0, −1.\n\nWhen distributing these modifiers, consider what actions you want your character to be good at, what weapon you want to use for your attacks, and what kind of spellcasting you’ll be doing, if any. Weapons and spells are covered in future sections, but the character guide for your class offers suggested placement for your modifiers if you want a starting point. You can also rearrange your modifiers as needed during character creation, as well as after your first few sessions of play.\n\nIf you ever need to refer to your trait modifiers as values (if a card or effect references your Movement, Cool, etc.) a +2 would be considered 2, and a −1 would still be considered −1. For example, if a domain card tells you to place a number of tokens equal to your character’s Intelligence on that card, and their Intelligence is +3, you would place 3 tokens on the card. If they have a negative modifier in that trait, you instead would place no tokens on the card (unless otherwise noted)."},{"n":4,"title":"Record Additional Character Information","text":"It’s time to take a quick break from making choices and instead fill out a few sections of your character sheet.\n\n#### EVASION\n\nYour character’s **Evasion** reflects how hard it is for adversaries to hit them. Your class’s starting Evasion appears right beneath the Evasion field on your character sheet; copy this number into the Evasion field. When an adversary makes an attack against your character, the GM rolls against their Evasion to see if the adversary succeeds. You can choose how your character’s Evasion manifests within the narrative—a wizard might avoid blows with shimmering arcane shields or bat away an adversary’s spells, while a ranger nimbly dodges out of the way of attacks and a warrior parries, blocks, and dodges.\n\nYour character’s Evasion starts low, but that’s because it’s not their only means of avoiding damage. If they’re hit, you’ll also have the opportunity to reduce the incoming damage using armor.\n\n#### HIT POINTS AND STRESS\n\nYour character’s health and well-being are represented by Hit Points and Stress. **Hit Points** (sometimes called HP) are an abstract reflection of your physical fortitude and ability to take hits from both blade and magic. Each class starts with a set number of Hit Points. **Stress** reflects your ability to withstand the pressures of dangerous situations and mental strain. Every class starts with 6 Stress.\n\nYou can describe your character’s Hit Points and Stress any way you wish, but they generally represent your character’s ability to get knocked down and keep coming back. You’ll mark your character’s Hit Points and Stress when these situations arise—and the fewer marks you have, the better.\n\n#### HOPE AND FEAR\n\n**Hope** and **Fear** are currencies used by the players and the GM to represent the way fate turns for or against the characters during the game. You start with 2 Hope; mark these in the Hope field of your character sheet.\n\nAny time you **roll with Hope** on your Duality Dice—meaning your Hope Die rolled higher than your Fear Die—you gain a Hope (to a maximum of 6). During the game, you can spend Hope to help your character’s allies, apply their life experiences to challenges, and empower their spells and abilities.\n\nIf you instead **roll with Fear** on your Duality Dice—meaning your Fear Die rolled higher than your Hope Die—the GM gains a Fear that they can spend on certain effects. Sometimes a Fear roll introduces other complications into a scene (even if you succeed on your roll)."},{"n":5,"title":"Choose Your Starting Equipment","text":"Next, you’ll choose starting weapons, armor, and other items for your character.\n\n#### CHOOSE YOUR WEAPONS\n\nYou use **physical weapons** to attack your foes—and if you have a Netrun trait (such as from your subclass), you can also wield **magic weapons**. At character creation, you can choose either a two-handed **primary weapon**, or a one-handed primary weapon and a one-handed **secondary weapon**, then equip them.\n\nYou can find weapon suggestions at the top of your character guide, but if you’d like to choose your own, you can find all starting (Tier 1) weapons in the “Primary Weapon Tables” and “Secondary Weapon Tables” sections in chapter 2. (These are also available as a separate sheet to print out from the Daggerheart website or in the appendix.)\n\nAt Level 1, your **Proficiency** is 1. This means you’ll roll one damage die for your weapon attacks. In the Active Weapons area of your character sheet, this is recorded in the Proficiency field.\n\n**Tip:** *In the area of your character labeled Damage Dice & Type, record your damage dice with the Proficiency value already written in (like “1d6+3” instead of “d6+3”), to remind yourself how many weapon dice to roll. Whenever you increase your Proficiency, you also increase the number of dice in the damage dice field to reflect this change. For example, when your Proficiency becomes 2, you would change it to \"2d6+3\" instead.*\n\n#### CHOOSE YOUR ARMOR\n\nYou use **armor** to reduce the severity of incoming attacks. At character creation, you can choose one set of armor and equip it. You can find armor suggestions at the top of your character guide, but if you’d like to choose your own, you can find all starting (Tier 1) armor in the “Armor Tables” section in chapter 2. (These are also available as a separate sheet to print out from the Daggerheart website or in the appendix.) When your character equips armor, record its details in the Active Armor fields of your character sheet.\n\nAn armor’s **damage thresholds** (shown in the “Base Thresholds” column) indicate how much damage a character can endure before marking Hit Points. Add your character’s level to both values and record the total for both numbers in the corresponding fields. In the “Hit Points and Damage Thresholds” section of chapter 2, you’ll learn how many Hit Points to mark on your character sheet each time your character takes damage.\n\nThen, in the Armor field at the top left of your character sheet, record your **Armor Score**. This score includes the armor’s base value (shown in the “Base Score” column) plus any permanent bonuses your character has to their Armor Score from other abilities. When your character takes damage, you can mark one of the small shield symbols next to your Armor Score (called an **Armor Slot**), then reduce the severity of the damage by one threshold: Severe to Major, Major to Minor, or Minor to None. You can only mark one Armor Slot per attack, and you have a number of slots equal to your Armor Score.\n\n#### CHOOSE OTHER STARTING ITEMS\n\nYour **inventory** includes anything else your character is carrying. The top of your character guide lists all their starting inventory items, which include the following:\n\n• Torch (useful for illuminating a dark room)\n\n• 50 feet of rope (useful for climbing a wall or rappelling down a cliff)\n\n• Basic supplies (tent, bedroll, tinderbox, rations, etc.)\n\n• A handful of gold (record this in the Gold field in the left-hand column of your character sheet)\n\n• Your choice of a Minor Health Potion (clear 1d4 Hit Points) or a Minor Stamina Potion (clear 1d4 Stress)\n\n• The “and either” option on your character guide is specific to your class. You might also be asked to choose what you use to carry your spells.\n\nRecord these items in the Inventory fields of your character sheet. This equipment is available to use during the party’s adventures in any way that fits the shared story. We encourage you to use items creatively, as long as the GM agrees and it fits the rules of the world.\n\nYou can also talk to your GM about other items you’d like to have at the start of the game. There are no rules limiting the size of your inventory, so items that don’t provide a mechanical benefit but make sense for your character to have are normally okay—but your GM always makes the final call."},{"n":6,"title":"Create Your Background","text":"Next, explore your character’s **background** by answering the background questions in your character guide. Several prompts are provided to jumpstart inspiration, but you or the GM can modify or replace these questions to fit the character you’re looking to play. The prompts are a jumping-off point if you don’t know where to start, but they should never inhibit your creativity.\n\nThe decisions you make about your character’s background are purely narrative, but they deeply impact the character you’re playing and the story the GM is preparing for your adventures. Over the course of character creation, feel free to adjust mechanical choices you made in earlier steps to better reflect this background as you shape your character.\n\nIf you’re planning to play in a campaign, you can continue developing your character after the background questions in whatever way works best for you. Just remember to give the GM your backstory so they can work any people, places, or ideas from it into the campaign. You can also choose not to do any more background work, instead finding out more about your character as you play. Lean into a process that’s fun for you."},{"n":7,"title":"Create Your Experiences","text":"In Daggerheart, your character’s Experiences are one of the core ways you express their backstory and expertise through mechanics. An **Experience** is a word or phrase used to encapsulate a specific set of skills your character has acquired over the course of their exciting life.\n\nYour character starts with two Experiences at character creation (each with a +2 modifier), and they’ll earn more throughout your adventures. Before creating Experiences, you’ll want to have a solid idea of who your character is; the background questions are especially helpful for this. When you’re ready, work with your GM to create two Experiences that have shaped your character.\n\nThere’s no set list of Experiences to choose from (though we offer options for inspiration under “Experience Examples”). Instead, find a word or phrase that embodies something distinctive about your character. Each Experience should be specific. For example, Talented or Focused are too broad, as they can be applied to almost any situation; instead you might use Swashbuckler or Magic Studies. Additionally, your character’s Experience can’t give them spells or specific game abilities. For example, Take Flight or One-Hit Kill are too mechanically oriented; you might consider Pilot or Assassin instead.\n\nYou are also encouraged to add flavor to your character’s Experience to give it more varied use in play. For example, instead of just Assassin, you could choose Assassin of the Sapphire Syndicate. Details like this give your GM an exciting faction to weave into the campaign, and also make it easier to use this Experience outside combat. For example, if your character encounters an ally of the syndicate, they might be particularly adept at negotiating with that NPC based on their Experience.\n\nOnce you’ve decided on your character’s two starting Experiences, record them in the Experience fields of your character sheet and assign +2 to both.\n\n**Tip:** *If you’re not sure what Experiences to take, consider the style of the campaign you’re playing in and the actions you’ll want to perform. In a standard, battle-focused campaign, it’s never a bad idea to take your character’s first Experience in something that will help with combat and the second Experience in something useful outside of combat.*\n\n> **EXPERIENCE EXAMPLES**\n> \n> **Backgrounds:** *Assassin, Blacksmith, Bodyguard, Bounty Hunter, Chef to the Royal Family, Circus Performer, Con Artist, Fallen Monarch, Field Medic, High Priestess, Merchant, Noble, Pirate, Politician, Runaway, Scholar, Sellsword, Soldier, Storyteller, Thief, World Traveler*\n> \n> **Characteristics:** *Affable, Battle-Hardened, Bookworm, Charming, Cowardly, Friend to All, Helpful, Intimidating Cool, Leader, Lone Wolf, Loyal, Observant, Prankster, Silver Tongue, Sticky Fingers, Stubborn to a Fault, Survivor, Young and Naive*\n> \n> **Specialties:** *Acrobat, Gambler, Healer, Inventor, Magical Historian, Mapmaker, Master of Disguise, Navigator, Sharpshooter, Survivalist, Swashbuckler, Tactician*\n> \n> **Skills:** *Animal Whisperer, Barter, Deadly Aim, Fast Learner, Incredible Body, Liar, Light Feet, Negotiator, Photographic Memory, Quick Hands, Repair, Scavenger, Tracker*\n> \n> **Phrases:** *Catch Me If You Can, Fake It Till You Make It, First Time’s the Charm, Hold the Line, I Won’t Let You Down, I’ll Catch You, I’ve Got Your Back, Intelligence Is Power, Nature’s Friend, Never Again, No One Left Behind, Pick on Someone Your Own Size, The Show Must Go On, This Is Not a Negotiation, Wolf in Sheep’s Clothing*\n\n#### USING EXPERIENCES\n\nWhen one of your character’s Experiences fits the situation at hand, you can use that Experience to showcase their expertise. Before you make an action or reaction roll, you can spend a Hope to add the Experience’s modifier to the roll’s result. Sometimes more than one of your character’s Experiences is a good fit for the situation (for example, if your character is trying to sneak past a guard in the dark, your I Live in the Shadows and Stealthy Experiences would both apply). If it fits the story, you can add more than one experience modifier to a roll, spending a Hope for each.\n\n#### CHANGING EXPERIENCES\n\nYour character has the opportunity to gain new Experiences as they gain levels. However, if you discover that your character has outgrown a previous Experience or it doesn’t feel right anymore, you’re not stuck with the ones you’ve already chosen. For example, you might find that your character’s Stealthy Experience no longer matches the head-on way they’ve learned to solve problems. Or perhaps your group likes to try to solve things diplomatically first, so your character’s Catch Them by Surprise Experience isn’t a good fit for the table’s collaborative story. That’s totally okay! Work with your GM to find Experiences that better align with your storytelling goals, and with the actions your character would be good at."},{"n":8,"title":"Choose Your Domain Cards","text":"**Domains** are the core building blocks of Daggerheart’s classes. The following domains are included in this book: Arcana, Blade, Bone, Codex, Grace, Midnight, Sage, Splendor, and Valor. Each domain has a **domain deck**—a deck of cards containing abilities and spells with a central theme or focus. For details on what each domain represents and how to use your domain cards, see the upcoming “Domains” section.\n\nEach class in Daggerheart is formed by combining two of these domains—for example, the warrior class comprises Blade and Bone, the druid comprises Arcana and Sage, and the rogue comprises Midnight and Grace. This combination is noted at the top of the character sheet for each class, and the colors and symbols are found on the subclass foundation card.\n\nTo start, look at all the level 1 cards from your class’s two domains and choose two cards, returning the rest to their respective decks. You can take one from each domain or two from a single domain, whichever you prefer. These cards grant you special spells and abilities; you’ll choose another card each time you level up.\n\n**Tip:** *All domain card text can also be found for reference on page 328.*\n\n#### SHARED DOMAINS\n\nEach class shares its domains with at least one other class. For example, Blade is shared by the guardian and the warrior, Sage is shared by the druid and the ranger, and Grace is shared by the bard and the rogue. If a fellow player’s class has the same domain as yours, we encourage you to coordinate with them and choose different cards from that domain deck (even if your group has multiple copies). This way, each player feels distinct and shines when they bring their unique abilities to the story. However, if the GM and players agree, feel free to make an exception. Sharing cards is common when more than one player chooses the same class or when three (or more) players share the same domain. As always, it comes down to open communication between the players and the GM. If you need extra copies of a card, you can download and print them at home."},{"n":9,"title":"Create Your Connections","text":"You’ve almost finished creating your character! Now it’s time to forge their **connections**. These represent the relationships and personal history between your character and their fellow party members.\n\nOnce your group is comfortable with their finished (or almostfinished) PCs, summarize your characters for each other. At minimum, share their name, pronouns, character description, Experiences, and the answers to the background questions— but feel free to include any details you’d like the other players to know.\n\nThen work together to decide how your characters are connected and how they feel about each other. The Connections section of your character guide provides inspiration—we recommend you pick at least one question to ask another player for their character to answer—but you’re welcome to create new questions. You can always turn down a question or relationship suggested by another player if it’s not a good fit or not a relationship you’re interested in exploring. While it’s great to create connections with every character, it’s perfectly okay if you’re not sure about some of them yet—and you can always discover your relationships through play. These connections are a starting point to build on during the game.\n\nAfter the party’s connections are complete, you’re ready to play! The rest of this chapter contains resources to use when building your character, such as information on domains, classes, ancestries, and communities. Chapter 2 then presents the rules you’ll use to play Daggerheart."}],"term_map":{"Spellcasting":"Quickhacking","Spellcast":"Quickhack","Agility":"Movement","Strength":"Body","Finesse":"Reflexes","Instinct":"Empathy","Presence":"Cool","Knowledge":"Intelligence","magic damage":"electronic damage","Spellcast Trait":"Netrun Trait","Spellcast trait":"Netrun trait","Wizard":"Netrunner","School of Knowledge":"Exfil","School of War":"Deletion","Warlock":"Netmerc","Pact of the Wrathful":"Bounty","Pact of the Endless":"Blackwall","Highborne":"North Oaks","highborne":"North Oaks","Loreborne":"Charter Hill","loreborne":"Charter Hill","Orderborne":"Arasaka Waterfront","orderborne":"Arasaka Waterfront","Ridgeborne":"Southern Badlands","ridgeborne":"Southern Badlands","Seaborne":"Wellsprings","seaborne":"Wellsprings","Slyborne":"Kabuki","slyborne":"Kabuki","Underborne":"Little China","underborne":"Little China","Wanderborne":"The Badlands","wanderborne":"The Badlands","Wildborne":"Northside","wildborne":"Northside","Duneborne":"Eastern Badlands","duneborne":"Eastern Badlands","Freeborne":"Pacifica","freeborne":"Pacifica","Frostborne":"Tycho, Lunar Colony","frostborne":"Tycho, Lunar Colony","Hearthborne":"The Glen","hearthborne":"The Glen","Reborne":"Corpo Plaza","reborne":"Corpo Plaza","Warborne":"Rancho Coronado","warborne":"Rancho Coronado","Troubadour":"Rockerboy","Wordsmith":"Media","Warden of the Elements":"Daemon Rider","Warden of Renewal":"Biohacker","Stalwart":"Bodyguard","Beastbound":"Nomad","Wayfinder":"Bounty Hunter","Nightwalker":"Infiltrator","Divine Wielder":"Medtech","Winged Sentinel":"Lawman","Elemental Origin":"Tech (Elementalist)","Primal Origin":"Tech (Overclock)","Call of the Brave":"Merc","Call of the Slayer":"Solo","Executioners Guild":"Hitman","Poisoners Guild":"Chemist"},"map":{"image":"Cyberpunk-2077-World-Map.webp","caption":"Night City — world map by game-maps.com"}};
